1
00:02:26,603 --> 00:02:29,882
♪ My royal day
can be a royal bore ♪

2
00:02:29,906 --> 00:02:33,119
♪ it leaves me colder
than a basement floor ♪

3
00:02:33,143 --> 00:02:36,455
♪ the only moment
I keep waiting for ♪

4
00:02:36,479 --> 00:02:40,059
♪ is when the day
will be through ♪

5
00:02:40,083 --> 00:02:43,429
♪ I never notice
if it's dark or clear ♪

6
00:02:43,453 --> 00:02:46,632
♪ what people say to me
I hardly hear ♪

7
00:02:46,656 --> 00:02:50,136
♪ the passing hours
are an endless year ♪

8
00:02:50,160 --> 00:02:56,461
♪ until at last
I'm alone with you ♪

9
00:03:00,637 --> 00:03:03,916
♪ every night at 7:00 ♪

10
00:03:03,940 --> 00:03:07,787
♪ you walk in
as fresh as clover ♪

11
00:03:07,811 --> 00:03:13,010
♪ and I begin to sigh
all over again ♪

12
00:03:14,684 --> 00:03:17,430
♪ every night at 7:00 ♪

13
00:03:17,454 --> 00:03:21,500
♪ you come by
like may returning ♪

14
00:03:21,524 --> 00:03:27,139
♪ and me, oh, my,
I start in yearning again ♪

15
00:03:27,163 --> 00:03:29,976
♪ you seem to bring ♪

16
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:32,011
♪ faraway spring ♪

17
00:03:32,035 --> 00:03:36,182
♪ near me ♪

18
00:03:36,206 --> 00:03:40,453
♪ I'm always
in full bloom... ♪

19
00:03:40,477 --> 00:03:42,622
♪ When you're
in the room ♪

20
00:03:42,646 --> 00:03:45,558
♪ for every night
at 7:00 ♪

21
00:03:45,582 --> 00:03:49,528
♪ every time
the same thing happens ♪

22
00:03:49,552 --> 00:03:51,864
♪ I fall once again
in love ♪

23
00:03:51,888 --> 00:03:55,620
♪ but only with you ♪

24
00:05:25,348 --> 00:05:29,395
♪ Every night at 7:00 ♪

25
00:05:29,419 --> 00:05:32,565
♪ every time
the same thing happens ♪

26
00:05:32,589 --> 00:05:34,967
♪ I fall once again
in love ♪

27
00:05:34,991 --> 00:05:39,824
♪ but only with you ♪

28
00:05:56,946 --> 00:05:58,991
May I say your performance
was a bit ragged?

29
00:05:59,015 --> 00:06:01,093
You were so slow going around the
throne, I almost caught you.

30
00:06:01,117 --> 00:06:03,162
Well, the audience seemed to like it.

31
00:06:03,186 --> 00:06:05,197
Whew! It's so hot in New York
in the summertime.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_York_City}

32
00:06:05,221 --> 00:06:06,766
I'm glad we're closing
tomorrow night.

33
00:06:06,790 --> 00:06:08,701
Wonder what happened
to the air conditioning.

34
00:06:08,725 --> 00:06:10,770
Mr. Hiller probably turned
it off to save money.

35
00:06:10,794 --> 00:06:12,705
Oh, would you blot my face, too?

36
00:06:12,729 --> 00:06:14,140
Up here.
It's trickling.

37
00:06:14,164 --> 00:06:15,641
And who's Mr. Hiller?

38
00:06:15,665 --> 00:06:17,777
The house manager.
You ought to know that.

39
00:06:17,801 --> 00:06:19,645
You've been playing his theater
for the past year and a half.

40
00:06:19,669 --> 00:06:21,647
Oh, is that that evil little man
who comes around and pinches?

41
00:06:21,671 --> 00:06:23,682
That's your department, sister dear.

42
00:06:23,706 --> 00:06:26,218
All I can say is let Mr. Hiller
keep his air conditioning.

43
00:06:26,242 --> 00:06:28,722
He can't take it with him,
and where he's going, he'll need it.

44
00:06:29,579 --> 00:06:31,791
Oh, boy, it's hot.
Say, if ever I play a king again,

45
00:06:31,815 --> 00:06:33,359
I'm going to be one
of those Asiatic boys

46
00:06:33,383 --> 00:06:35,194
who just wears earrings and a sheet.

47
00:06:35,218 --> 00:06:37,329
Hey, what's your hurry?

48
00:06:37,353 --> 00:06:38,130
Pete.

49
00:06:38,154 --> 00:06:39,465
Hi, Ellen honey.

50
00:06:39,489 --> 00:06:40,833
I'm sorry to keep you waiting.

51
00:06:40,857 --> 00:06:42,735
You know my brother Tom, don't you?

52
00:06:42,759 --> 00:06:44,170
No, I don't believe
I've had the pleasure.

53
00:06:44,194 --> 00:06:45,838
How are you?

54
00:06:45,862 --> 00:06:46,906
I'm fine, sir,
and thank you for asking.

55
00:06:46,930 --> 00:06:48,340
I'll be out in 5.

56
00:06:48,364 --> 00:06:49,975
Oh, my, Pete, you look
so nice and cool.

57
00:06:49,999 --> 00:06:51,310
Make yourself at home, won't you?

58
00:06:51,334 --> 00:06:53,546
Sit down anywhere.

59
00:06:53,570 --> 00:06:55,414
Oh, how was it, sir?

60
00:06:55,438 --> 00:06:57,016
Hot.

61
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:59,518
Oh, your agent stopped
by to see you, sir.

62
00:06:59,542 --> 00:07:02,087
Irving? What brought him out
this clammy night?

63
00:07:02,111 --> 00:07:04,890
He didn't say. He just told me
to tell you it was imperative

64
00:07:04,914 --> 00:07:06,759
that you and miss Ellen
meet him at McGuffy's bar

65
00:07:06,783 --> 00:07:08,360
next door right after
the performance.

66
00:07:08,384 --> 00:07:10,095
Oh. Ellen.

67
00:07:10,119 --> 00:07:11,497
Yeah.

68
00:07:11,521 --> 00:07:13,732
Irving wants us to meet him
at McGuffy's.

69
00:07:13,756 --> 00:07:16,702
OK. How'd you like Pete?
Isn't he cute? He's from the south.

70
00:07:16,726 --> 00:07:19,171
No.
Why, Ellen honey,

71
00:07:19,195 --> 00:07:20,873
I never would've guessed it.

72
00:07:20,897 --> 00:07:22,608
He owns miles and miles
of tobacco land in Virginia,

73
00:07:22,632 --> 00:07:24,210
and you ought to hear him chant.

74
00:07:24,234 --> 00:07:26,045
I met him 2 days ago
at Hilda swan's house,

75
00:07:26,069 --> 00:07:27,546
and I'm simply mad about him.
Is that all?

76
00:07:27,570 --> 00:07:29,248
That's all.
Roger.

77
00:07:29,272 --> 00:07:31,951
Miss Ellen seems quite
taken, doesn't she?

78
00:07:31,975 --> 00:07:35,087
This will probably be one of those long
affairs that drags on a whole weekend.

79
00:07:35,111 --> 00:07:36,455
The big news overseas

80
00:07:36,479 --> 00:07:38,090
concerns the royal wedding in London.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London}

81
00:07:38,114 --> 00:07:40,593
The British capital is
already festive and happy

82
00:07:40,617 --> 00:07:42,595
in anticipation of the wedding,
which is still several weeks off.

83
00:07:42,619 --> 00:07:44,697
Towel.

84
00:07:44,721 --> 00:07:46,866
As for the Princess,
the most closely guarded secret since radar

85
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:48,701
is her wedding gown.
The Princess herself

86
00:07:48,725 --> 00:07:50,436
will see it for the
first time this weekend.

87
00:07:50,460 --> 00:07:51,904
Please, Mr. Bowen.
Chester, I...

88
00:07:51,928 --> 00:07:53,372
in other news, in Miami, Florida,{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miami}

89
00:07:53,396 --> 00:07:54,640
a new tropical hurricane

90
00:07:54,664 --> 00:07:55,875
seems to be approaching...

91
00:07:55,899 --> 00:07:57,409
can I talk now?
Yes, sir.

92
00:07:57,433 --> 00:07:58,744
You know, Mr. Bowen,

93
00:07:58,768 --> 00:08:00,112
I danced in Trafalgar square

94
00:08:00,136 --> 00:08:01,576
the day her grandfather was married.

95
00:08:02,505 --> 00:08:03,315
I'd give anything to be
there again this fall,

96
00:08:03,339 --> 00:08:04,116
wouldn't you?

97
00:08:04,140 --> 00:08:05,417
Yes. I like weddings,

98
00:08:05,441 --> 00:08:06,785
provided, of course,
they're not mine.

99
00:08:06,809 --> 00:08:08,787
Oh, marriages are very healthy, sir.

100
00:08:08,811 --> 00:08:11,624
They say married men live
much longer than bachelors.

101
00:08:11,648 --> 00:08:13,092
If that's true, they're only
trying to outlive their wives

102
00:08:13,116 --> 00:08:14,827
so they can be bachelors again.

103
00:08:14,851 --> 00:08:16,762
Haven't you ever thought
of getting married, sir?

104
00:08:16,786 --> 00:08:18,297
Once, as a matter of fact, I did.

105
00:08:18,321 --> 00:08:20,599
The young lady changed her
mind at the last moment.

106
00:08:20,623 --> 00:08:22,434
I've been deeply indebted
to her ever since.

107
00:08:22,458 --> 00:08:23,736
Good night, Chester.

108
00:08:23,760 --> 00:08:26,572
Gee, I wonder what Irving wants.

109
00:08:26,596 --> 00:08:28,140
I hope it's something that...

110
00:08:28,164 --> 00:08:30,609
my gosh, what a muscle.
It's like cement.

111
00:08:30,633 --> 00:08:33,445
Oh, it's nothing.
All us tobacco men get kind of strong.

112
00:08:33,469 --> 00:08:34,446
What from?

113
00:08:34,470 --> 00:08:37,249
Lifting money.

114
00:08:37,273 --> 00:08:39,985
Come now, it wasn't that good.

115
00:08:40,009 --> 00:08:41,186
Good night, Eddie.
Good night.

116
00:08:41,210 --> 00:08:43,188
Good night, Eddie.
Good night.

117
00:08:43,212 --> 00:08:47,578
He's the only stage door man
I know who isn't called pop.

118
00:08:49,686 --> 00:08:51,363
Hello, will.
Gee, it's been a long time.

119
00:08:51,387 --> 00:08:52,932
Hi, Irv.

120
00:08:52,956 --> 00:08:54,867
Oh, I couldn't wait for you
back stage tonight.

121
00:08:54,891 --> 00:08:57,269
What is with that heat over there?
Who needs it? So how was the show?

122
00:08:57,293 --> 00:08:58,804
Well, the first act was a little...

123
00:08:58,828 --> 00:09:01,340
doll boat, how are you, baby?
Fine. How are you?

124
00:09:01,364 --> 00:09:02,708
You look so sweet tonight.

125
00:09:02,732 --> 00:09:04,209
Ooh, I do, don't I?

126
00:09:04,233 --> 00:09:06,111
OK, come on, order.
I got news, big news.

127
00:09:06,135 --> 00:09:08,180
Oh, Irving, can I present Pete Cumberly?
Let's have it.

128
00:09:08,204 --> 00:09:09,548
Well, how do you do, Mr. Cumberly?
It's a great pleasure.

129
00:09:09,572 --> 00:09:10,716
Thank you, sir.
That's very nice.

130
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:11,717
Who is this square?

131
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:12,685
Friend.
Tom Collins, please.

132
00:09:12,709 --> 00:09:14,219
Nothing for me, thank you.

133
00:09:14,243 --> 00:09:15,287
Hit me with a rot.
Shall I give it to you?

134
00:09:15,311 --> 00:09:17,723
Well, come on.
What is it?

135
00:09:17,747 --> 00:09:19,792
This'll kill you. My brother
Edgar called tonight from England.

136
00:09:19,816 --> 00:09:21,827
He's the head of our London office.
Been there for years.

137
00:09:21,851 --> 00:09:23,963
The Mayfair theater people want
your whole show for London.

138
00:09:23,987 --> 00:09:25,531
No.

139
00:09:25,555 --> 00:09:27,066
But quick. They want you there
during the wedding season.

140
00:09:27,090 --> 00:09:28,367
England during the wedding.

141
00:09:28,391 --> 00:09:30,169
I like that.
Oh, Tommy.

142
00:09:30,193 --> 00:09:32,171
Ellen honey, does this mean
you'll be leaving me?

143
00:09:32,195 --> 00:09:34,239
Oh, yes, Pete. Isn't it wonderful?
When do we open?

144
00:09:34,263 --> 00:09:36,408
As soon as possible. Of course,
you got to rehearse the English cast first.

145
00:09:36,432 --> 00:09:37,376
Hit me, son.

146
00:09:37,400 --> 00:09:39,178
What about transportation?

147
00:09:39,202 --> 00:09:40,346
There's some French boat
leaving a week from next Monday.

148
00:09:40,370 --> 00:09:41,680
Which one?

149
00:09:41,704 --> 00:09:43,082
Who knows what the name?
It's in French.

150
00:09:43,106 --> 00:09:45,084
Don't worry.
I'll get you on it.

151
00:09:45,108 --> 00:09:47,419
Ellen, this means we'll be
saying good-bye in 10 days.

152
00:09:47,443 --> 00:09:49,088
Oh, yes, Pete, and I'm so happy.

153
00:09:49,112 --> 00:09:49,922
Ellen.

154
00:09:49,946 --> 00:09:51,857
Oh, hello, Dick.

155
00:09:51,881 --> 00:09:53,325
I was waiting for you back stage.
That's why I'm late.

156
00:09:53,349 --> 00:09:55,027
Ellen, who is this fella?

157
00:09:55,051 --> 00:09:57,529
Finish your drink, Pete.
Did you hear the good news?

158
00:09:57,553 --> 00:09:59,598
We're going to England,
and we're going to be there during the wedding.

159
00:09:59,622 --> 00:10:00,699
What are you doing here, sir?

160
00:10:00,723 --> 00:10:02,067
What do you mean?
Who's he?

161
00:10:02,091 --> 00:10:04,003
A friend.
What are you doing here, sir?

162
00:10:04,027 --> 00:10:05,437
I demand an answer.

163
00:10:05,461 --> 00:10:06,372
Go peddle your papers,
will you? When do you leave?

164
00:10:06,396 --> 00:10:07,940
That's an insult.

165
00:10:07,964 --> 00:10:09,875
What's an insult?
What's the matter with you?

166
00:10:09,899 --> 00:10:12,745
This is terribly embarrassing.
I forgot all about Dick.

167
00:10:12,769 --> 00:10:13,946
Hope they don't hurt each other.

168
00:10:13,970 --> 00:10:15,748
I think we ought to start packing.

169
00:10:15,772 --> 00:10:16,749
I have to buy all my new
clothes before I go over.

170
00:10:16,773 --> 00:10:18,917
You'll have time.

171
00:10:18,941 --> 00:10:20,352
I'll send the rest of the company
over the week after you leave.

172
00:10:20,376 --> 00:10:21,787
I wonder what they'll be wearing.

173
00:10:21,811 --> 00:10:23,255
I don't know. Pay the man,
will you, Irving?

174
00:10:23,279 --> 00:10:24,356
Yeah, sure.
How much do we owe you?

175
00:10:24,380 --> 00:10:25,724
May I?
Yes, you may.

176
00:10:25,748 --> 00:10:28,012
And keep the change.

177
00:10:34,157 --> 00:10:36,268
I have a little business to take care of.
I'll meet you on deck.

178
00:10:36,292 --> 00:10:39,123
OK, sister dear.

179
00:10:40,163 --> 00:10:42,141
Hello, Billy.
This is good-bye.

180
00:10:42,165 --> 00:10:44,209
Thanks for coming down to see me off.

181
00:10:44,233 --> 00:10:45,644
It's hard saying good-bye like this

182
00:10:45,668 --> 00:10:47,613
after all we've been to each other.

183
00:10:47,637 --> 00:10:51,083
Oh, I know. It's been a lovely 3
days, but I'll be back soon.

184
00:10:51,107 --> 00:10:52,885
Oh, dear, I must go now.

185
00:10:52,909 --> 00:10:54,319
May I walk you up the gangplank?

186
00:10:54,343 --> 00:10:56,789
I'd rather you wouldn't.
You understand?

187
00:10:56,813 --> 00:10:58,257
No.

188
00:10:58,281 --> 00:11:00,726
Well, it's just better that way.

189
00:11:00,750 --> 00:11:02,961
Write to me, Billy.

190
00:11:02,985 --> 00:11:05,164
I will.
Good-bye, Ellen.

191
00:11:05,188 --> 00:11:07,499
I'll miss you very much, Linda.

192
00:11:07,523 --> 00:11:09,802
I can tell you that knowing
you these past few weeks

193
00:11:09,826 --> 00:11:12,337
has been one of the most exciting
things that's ever happened to me.

194
00:11:12,361 --> 00:11:14,558
To me, too, John.

195
00:11:23,339 --> 00:11:24,850
Pete!

196
00:11:24,874 --> 00:11:28,187
Ellen honey,
I thought I went to the wrong deck.

197
00:11:28,211 --> 00:11:29,354
You haven't much time.

198
00:11:29,378 --> 00:11:31,924
Really? And I had
so much to say.

199
00:11:31,948 --> 00:11:33,092
What?

200
00:11:33,116 --> 00:11:34,293
Well, you're gonna be away...

201
00:11:34,317 --> 00:11:35,828
yes, I know, Pete, but what?

202
00:11:35,852 --> 00:11:37,696
And suddenly knowing
you're gonna be away

203
00:11:37,720 --> 00:11:39,798
makes me realize my feelings for you,

204
00:11:39,822 --> 00:11:43,135
so I thought I'd...
I mean... Ellen honey...

205
00:11:43,159 --> 00:11:44,703
visitors ashore, please.

206
00:11:44,727 --> 00:11:46,038
You'll have to go now.
Good-bye.

207
00:11:46,062 --> 00:11:47,606
But, Ellen honey...

208
00:11:47,630 --> 00:11:48,674
you better hurry. Write me
what you had to say, huh?

209
00:11:48,698 --> 00:11:49,875
I will.
Bye.

210
00:11:49,899 --> 00:11:51,210
I can tell you, Barbara,

211
00:11:51,234 --> 00:11:53,512
that knowing you these past few weeks

212
00:11:53,536 --> 00:11:55,180
has been one of the most exciting
things that's ever happened to me.

213
00:11:55,204 --> 00:11:57,167
Oh, I'll miss you, John.

214
00:12:03,579 --> 00:12:05,023
Good-bye, baby.

215
00:12:05,047 --> 00:12:06,225
Good-bye, Irv.

216
00:12:06,249 --> 00:12:08,060
I know you'll be a smash.

217
00:12:08,084 --> 00:12:09,862
Now, if you want anything,
you ask my brother Edgar.

218
00:12:09,886 --> 00:12:11,029
He's gonna meet you at the hotel.

219
00:12:11,053 --> 00:12:12,030
So long, Irv.

220
00:12:12,054 --> 00:12:13,484
Bye.

221
00:12:16,592 --> 00:12:18,904
So, listen, have a good time.

222
00:12:18,928 --> 00:12:20,472
So long, Ellen.
So long.

223
00:12:20,496 --> 00:12:23,694
Bye, honey. Bye.
See you soon.

224
00:12:26,602 --> 00:12:28,747
So long.
See you.

225
00:12:28,771 --> 00:12:30,315
Hey, who you waving at up there?

226
00:12:30,339 --> 00:12:31,917
That girl right there, see?

227
00:12:31,941 --> 00:12:33,485
That's my girl.

228
00:12:33,509 --> 00:12:35,821
Don't give me that.
That's my girl.

229
00:12:38,548 --> 00:12:41,994
I'm betting 8 to 5 on Dixie boy.

230
00:12:42,018 --> 00:12:44,648
Wait a minute.
Wait a minute. This is...

231
00:12:45,721 --> 00:12:47,099
fellas, now look...

232
00:12:48,124 --> 00:12:49,268
Poor Irv.

233
00:12:49,292 --> 00:12:52,523
Hold it. Now listen...

234
00:13:10,980 --> 00:13:12,057
What a beautiful, beautiful day.

235
00:13:12,081 --> 00:13:13,725
Isn't it wonderful?

236
00:13:13,749 --> 00:13:15,027
We don't know a soul on the boat,

237
00:13:15,051 --> 00:13:16,461
and we can have fun
together for a change.

238
00:13:16,485 --> 00:13:17,462
Yeah.

239
00:13:17,486 --> 00:13:18,463
Good day.

240
00:13:18,487 --> 00:13:20,933
Good day.

241
00:13:20,957 --> 00:13:22,701
Oh, well, I don't know him, Tom.

242
00:13:24,126 --> 00:13:26,405
Honest, I don't.
I want to write a letter.

243
00:13:26,429 --> 00:13:28,240
OK, baby. Don't forget,
we've got to rehearse.

244
00:13:28,264 --> 00:13:29,641
What time shall I meet you?

245
00:13:29,665 --> 00:13:31,410
2:00 sharp in the gym.
I won't keep you long.

246
00:13:31,434 --> 00:13:32,377
I'll be there.
Don't worry.

247
00:13:32,401 --> 00:13:33,864
Now don't be late.
OK.

248
00:13:46,782 --> 00:13:48,160
Good day.

249
00:13:48,184 --> 00:13:49,895
Good day.

250
00:13:49,919 --> 00:13:52,130
There's carbon paper
in the middle drawer

251
00:13:52,154 --> 00:13:53,765
if you want to make duplicates.

252
00:13:53,789 --> 00:13:56,068
I'm not writing a letter.
I was just doodling,

253
00:13:56,092 --> 00:13:58,637
waiting for an opportunity
to introduce myself.

254
00:13:58,661 --> 00:14:02,174
Uh, my name is Brindale,
Lord John Brindale.

255
00:14:02,198 --> 00:14:04,009
And what's yours?

256
00:14:04,033 --> 00:14:06,345
Bowen, duchess Agatha Bowen.

257
00:14:06,369 --> 00:14:08,080
Oh, no, no.
Really, what is it?

258
00:14:08,104 --> 00:14:10,649
Let's see, now.
"E.B."

259
00:14:10,673 --> 00:14:12,017
"E."
is that Ethel?

260
00:14:12,041 --> 00:14:15,020
Wait a minute.

261
00:14:15,044 --> 00:14:17,289
Bowen. Why, of course.
You're Ellen Bowen, aren't you?

262
00:14:17,313 --> 00:14:18,991
You're doing fine.

263
00:14:19,015 --> 00:14:21,093
It's very stupid of me.
I should've known.

264
00:14:21,117 --> 00:14:22,594
Why, I only saw your
show a few weeks ago.

265
00:14:22,618 --> 00:14:24,363
Please forgive me.

266
00:14:24,387 --> 00:14:27,633
Oh, that's all right.
I know you have a lot on your mind.

267
00:14:27,657 --> 00:14:30,669
Oh, I can explain
those 2 little episodes

268
00:14:30,693 --> 00:14:32,271
that you witnessed yesterday.

269
00:14:32,295 --> 00:14:33,872
Oh, you can?
Yes.

270
00:14:33,896 --> 00:14:34,873
Then go ahead.

271
00:14:34,897 --> 00:14:36,875
Go ahead.

272
00:14:36,899 --> 00:14:38,277
Well, you see...

273
00:14:38,301 --> 00:14:39,778
They're both terribly nice girls,

274
00:14:39,802 --> 00:14:41,113
and they're both so fond of me

275
00:14:41,137 --> 00:14:42,314
that I... well,
I couldn't bear

276
00:14:42,338 --> 00:14:44,116
depriving either one of them.

277
00:14:44,140 --> 00:14:46,351
You know how it is.
In order to be kind to people,

278
00:14:46,375 --> 00:14:48,453
one has to be a very, very good liar.

279
00:14:48,477 --> 00:14:51,123
T-tell me, why were you following me?

280
00:14:51,147 --> 00:14:53,959
Following you?
I was doing no such thing.

281
00:14:53,983 --> 00:14:56,295
I was only saying good-bye
to a couple of...

282
00:14:56,319 --> 00:14:57,696
the same?

283
00:14:57,720 --> 00:14:59,965
Yes.

284
00:14:59,989 --> 00:15:02,868
Does this sort of thing
happen to you very often?

285
00:15:02,892 --> 00:15:04,303
All the time. And you?

286
00:15:04,327 --> 00:15:05,737
Constantly.

287
00:15:05,761 --> 00:15:08,240
I guess we're both 2 kind people.
Yeah.

288
00:15:08,264 --> 00:15:11,376
I have to get a stamp.
Do you live in New York?

289
00:15:11,400 --> 00:15:13,378
No. I've been there on
business for some time,

290
00:15:13,402 --> 00:15:14,913
but I'm just going home
now for the wedding.

291
00:15:14,937 --> 00:15:16,214
The royal wedding?
Yes.

292
00:15:16,238 --> 00:15:17,916
You're going to the wedding?

293
00:15:17,940 --> 00:15:19,851
I'm escorting someone, yes.

294
00:15:19,875 --> 00:15:22,054
You really are a lord, aren't you?

295
00:15:22,078 --> 00:15:22,988
I'm afraid so.

296
00:15:23,012 --> 00:15:24,990
My, my.

297
00:15:25,014 --> 00:15:26,925
Haven't you ever met anyone
who held a title before?

298
00:15:26,949 --> 00:15:29,394
Only Joe Louis.

299
00:15:29,418 --> 00:15:31,530
Do you know I'm very glad
that you're Ellen Bowen?

300
00:15:31,554 --> 00:15:33,098
Why?

301
00:15:33,122 --> 00:15:34,499
Because that means
that chap you're with

302
00:15:34,523 --> 00:15:35,901
is your brother and not your husband.

303
00:15:35,925 --> 00:15:36,935
Do you know what I did last night?

304
00:15:36,959 --> 00:15:38,537
No. What?

305
00:15:38,561 --> 00:15:40,372
I surveyed this whole boat,
and I discovered

306
00:15:40,396 --> 00:15:41,673
that you were the most
attractive girl on board.

307
00:15:41,697 --> 00:15:43,675
Well, thank you.

308
00:15:43,699 --> 00:15:46,211
And I kept thinking that "what
a pity it is she's married,

309
00:15:46,235 --> 00:15:49,014
because that's going to make
things terribly awkward."

310
00:15:49,038 --> 00:15:52,517
But it isn't going to be
awkward at all now.

311
00:15:52,541 --> 00:15:53,618
Thanks for the use
of the hall, purser.

312
00:15:53,642 --> 00:15:54,853
Not at all.

313
00:15:54,877 --> 00:15:56,621
If you see my sister on deck,

314
00:15:56,645 --> 00:15:57,756
will you tell her I'm waiting for
her, please?

315
00:15:57,780 --> 00:15:58,790
I will.
I like your boat.

316
00:15:58,814 --> 00:16:00,125
Thank you, monsieur.

317
00:16:00,149 --> 00:16:02,260
Oh, monsieur Bowen,
I have a favor to ask.

318
00:16:02,284 --> 00:16:06,064
On every crossing, you know,
we have a gala benefit for disabled seamen,

319
00:16:06,088 --> 00:16:07,532
and I was wondering if you and
your sister would dance for us

320
00:16:07,556 --> 00:16:08,934
on that night.

321
00:16:08,958 --> 00:16:10,802
Oh, we'd love to.
Just tell us when.

322
00:16:10,826 --> 00:16:12,971
Just one number would be sufficient.
We don't want to ask...

323
00:16:12,995 --> 00:16:14,940
no trouble at all.

324
00:16:14,964 --> 00:16:17,542
I know it's an imposition to ask you
two, but I would feel...

325
00:16:17,566 --> 00:16:20,764
we'd love to. Don't worry.
We'll do it.

326
00:16:26,742 --> 00:16:30,073
There's a fella that won't
take yes for an answer.

327
00:20:41,330 --> 00:20:43,408
Oh, fine. Thanks for
showing up at rehearsals.

328
00:20:43,432 --> 00:20:47,479
Oh. Oh, John, this is my brother Tom.
Tom, this is John.

329
00:20:47,503 --> 00:20:48,980
How do you do?
Glad to know you.

330
00:20:49,004 --> 00:20:50,315
I just picked up John
in the writing room.

331
00:20:50,339 --> 00:20:52,183
So I gathered.
He's a real lord.

332
00:20:52,207 --> 00:20:53,718
Oh, it's nothing at all.

333
00:20:53,742 --> 00:20:54,586
Miss Bowen.
Yes?

334
00:20:54,610 --> 00:20:55,887
Cable.

335
00:20:55,911 --> 00:20:58,475
Oh, thank you.
Excuse me, please.

336
00:21:01,784 --> 00:21:04,663
Oh, Tom... Tom,
it's from Pete.

337
00:21:04,687 --> 00:21:08,099
Says he's very blue,
except for his eye, which is black.

338
00:21:08,123 --> 00:21:11,236
Uh, let's forget Pete
for this trip, shall we?

339
00:21:11,260 --> 00:21:13,089
Excuse us.

340
00:21:24,440 --> 00:21:25,383
Looks a little grim out there.

341
00:21:25,407 --> 00:21:27,285
Yeah.
It does.

342
00:21:27,309 --> 00:21:29,254
Monsieur Bowen,
I'm wondering if you and your sister

343
00:21:29,278 --> 00:21:31,256
will dance right away instead
of waiting until 10:30.

344
00:21:31,280 --> 00:21:32,924
We're a little afraid of the weather.

345
00:21:32,948 --> 00:21:34,526
You mean, it might be
a little... Rocky?

346
00:21:34,550 --> 00:21:35,894
Yes.

347
00:21:35,918 --> 00:21:37,195
That won't bother us.
We can handle it.

348
00:21:37,219 --> 00:21:38,730
Thank you, monsieur.
Any time you say.

349
00:21:38,754 --> 00:21:40,884
I better change my dress.

350
00:21:50,065 --> 00:21:53,712
♪ Open your eyes ♪

351
00:21:53,736 --> 00:21:58,116
♪ there's a sapphire sky
above us ♪

352
00:21:58,140 --> 00:22:00,719
♪ high above us ♪

353
00:22:00,743 --> 00:22:04,055
♪ made for you ♪

354
00:22:04,079 --> 00:22:11,296
♪ when you open your eyes ♪

355
00:22:11,320 --> 00:22:14,599
♪ open your eyes ♪

356
00:22:14,623 --> 00:22:19,137
♪ there's a carpet
of Jade around us ♪

357
00:22:19,161 --> 00:22:21,506
♪ laid around us ♪

358
00:22:21,530 --> 00:22:24,909
♪ all for you ♪

359
00:22:24,933 --> 00:22:32,650
♪ when you open your eyes ♪

360
00:22:32,674 --> 00:22:37,922
♪ let me
show you the sights ♪

361
00:22:37,946 --> 00:22:41,159
♪ take you ♪

362
00:22:41,183 --> 00:22:47,165
♪ on a tour
of this great, new ♪

363
00:22:47,189 --> 00:22:52,704
♪ fabulous world
we own ♪

364
00:22:52,728 --> 00:22:57,709
♪ we alone ♪

365
00:22:57,733 --> 00:23:01,279
♪ so open your eyes ♪

366
00:23:01,303 --> 00:23:06,518
♪ and you'll see
how this momentary ♪

367
00:23:06,542 --> 00:23:10,522
♪ ordinary ♪

368
00:23:10,546 --> 00:23:15,727
♪ night can seem ♪

369
00:23:15,751 --> 00:23:23,019
♪ more unreal than a dream ♪

370
00:24:34,830 --> 00:24:36,808
We can handle it, all right.

371
00:24:36,832 --> 00:24:38,076
What do we do now?

372
00:24:38,100 --> 00:24:39,110
Keep your balance.

373
00:24:39,134 --> 00:24:40,930
Oh, sure.

374
00:24:52,414 --> 00:24:54,411
So sorry.

375
00:24:55,918 --> 00:24:57,747
Pardon us.

376
00:25:59,314 --> 00:26:01,144
What?

377
00:26:29,511 --> 00:26:31,841
Isn't it wonderful, Tommy?
It sure is.

378
00:26:36,218 --> 00:26:39,731
Hello? Yes? Oh, sure.

379
00:26:39,755 --> 00:26:43,201
Yes. Do come on up.
Room 211, 12, and 14.

380
00:26:43,225 --> 00:26:44,202
OK.

381
00:26:44,226 --> 00:26:45,870
Who was that?

382
00:26:45,894 --> 00:26:49,440
Edgar Klinger, Irving's brother.
He's coming up.

383
00:26:49,464 --> 00:26:51,576
I'm so happy, Tommy.

384
00:26:51,600 --> 00:26:54,379
I wonder what the Princess
is doing this morning.

385
00:26:54,403 --> 00:26:56,414
Why don't you call her and ask her?

386
00:26:56,438 --> 00:26:58,683
Wonder what I'd be doing
a month before my wedding.

387
00:26:58,707 --> 00:27:00,251
Probably trying to figure
a way to get out of it.

388
00:27:00,275 --> 00:27:01,786
Do you really think so?

389
00:27:01,810 --> 00:27:03,673
You know you would, and so would I.

390
00:27:08,483 --> 00:27:11,462
How do you do?
I'm Edgar Klinger.

391
00:27:12,821 --> 00:27:14,198
We didn't know...

392
00:27:14,222 --> 00:27:15,199
That Irving and I were twins?

393
00:27:15,223 --> 00:27:17,035
No, we didn't.

394
00:27:17,059 --> 00:27:19,270
Oh, I must say,
he should have told you, you know.

395
00:27:19,294 --> 00:27:22,273
After all, this is a pretty box of pickles.
May I come in?

396
00:27:22,297 --> 00:27:24,809
Well, of course.
I'm sorry.

397
00:27:24,833 --> 00:27:26,144
How do you do?

398
00:27:26,168 --> 00:27:28,279
So happy to meet you at long last.

399
00:27:28,303 --> 00:27:29,547
My, it's amazing.

400
00:27:29,571 --> 00:27:30,915
Yes, it is rather, isn't it?

401
00:27:30,939 --> 00:27:32,917
I do hope the rooms are satisfactory.

402
00:27:32,941 --> 00:27:35,153
Oh, they're fine.
It's a miracle you could get them.

403
00:27:35,177 --> 00:27:37,488
Yes, quite. The wedding
has packed things up a bit,

404
00:27:37,512 --> 00:27:39,991
but, uh, then there are ways.

405
00:27:40,015 --> 00:27:40,992
How's everything in the theater?

406
00:27:41,016 --> 00:27:43,294
Oh, seems to be humming.

407
00:27:43,318 --> 00:27:46,266
The dancers are coming to audition this
afternoon, the singers tomorrow.

408
00:27:46,655 --> 00:27:48,885
Excuse me.

409
00:27:49,624 --> 00:27:50,968
Hello.

410
00:27:50,992 --> 00:27:53,004
Oh, hello, Johnny.
How are you?

411
00:27:53,028 --> 00:27:56,240
Ellen's boat romance: John brindale.
Do you know him?

412
00:27:56,264 --> 00:27:58,576
Know the family.
Very old.

413
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:01,279
They do say that young
John is a bit of a chaser.

414
00:28:01,303 --> 00:28:05,683
Didn't have to chase very hard after Ellen.
She stood still and waited.

415
00:28:05,707 --> 00:28:08,419
This afternoon?
No, they're auditioning all day.

416
00:28:08,443 --> 00:28:11,756
Just a minute.
Tom, you don't need me this afternoon, do you?

417
00:28:11,780 --> 00:28:13,291
John wants me to drive down
and see his old country home.

418
00:28:13,315 --> 00:28:15,593
I suppose it's all right,

419
00:28:15,617 --> 00:28:17,528
but I should think you'd want to come
along and see who you're working with.

420
00:28:17,552 --> 00:28:19,997
Well, I never notice anyone but you.
John, I...

421
00:28:20,021 --> 00:28:21,299
I want you home for dinner.

422
00:28:21,323 --> 00:28:23,134
And no dates at night
until after we open.

423
00:28:23,158 --> 00:28:24,936
You'll come strolling in
at 4:00 in the morning

424
00:28:24,960 --> 00:28:26,871
and be all tired out next day.

425
00:28:26,895 --> 00:28:30,374
Yes, papa. All set.
What time will you pick me up?

426
00:28:30,398 --> 00:28:33,311
OK. I'll be ready.
Bye, John.

427
00:28:33,335 --> 00:28:34,645
Shall we?

428
00:28:34,669 --> 00:28:36,948
Yeah. See you at 7:00.
And be back.

429
00:28:36,972 --> 00:28:37,648
Oh, I will.
I will.

430
00:28:37,672 --> 00:28:38,449
Cheerio.

431
00:28:38,473 --> 00:28:40,284
Good-bye, Edgar.

432
00:28:40,308 --> 00:28:42,120
Oh, and if there's anything you
need, do call.

433
00:28:42,144 --> 00:28:42,954
Thank you.

434
00:28:42,978 --> 00:28:44,989
After you.

435
00:28:45,013 --> 00:28:47,658
Tell me, old boy,
how are things in the colonies these days?

436
00:28:47,682 --> 00:28:49,646
Oh, fine. Fine.

437
00:28:56,992 --> 00:28:59,303
These English clothes are terrific.

438
00:28:59,327 --> 00:29:00,705
Frightfully amusing tie, what?

439
00:29:00,729 --> 00:29:02,191
Yeah.

440
00:29:05,033 --> 00:29:07,163
Got a match?

441
00:29:09,437 --> 00:29:12,016
Thanks. I hear that shaving
lotion's great stuff.

442
00:29:12,040 --> 00:29:13,151
Did you ever use it?

443
00:29:13,175 --> 00:29:15,019
Oh, every day.

444
00:29:15,043 --> 00:29:17,755
Sorry. I thought
you were him.

445
00:29:17,779 --> 00:29:20,258
How odd. Thank you.

446
00:29:20,282 --> 00:29:22,927
Well, I must get back to the office.

447
00:29:22,951 --> 00:29:24,562
You know where the theater
is, don't you? Sure.

448
00:29:24,586 --> 00:29:27,350
Well, I'll see you later.
Cheerio. OK.

449
00:29:53,949 --> 00:29:55,626
Hello again.

450
00:29:55,650 --> 00:29:57,762
I wish you'd stop following me.

451
00:29:57,786 --> 00:29:58,796
Following you?

452
00:29:58,820 --> 00:30:01,050
I'm not a bit flattered.

453
00:30:22,978 --> 00:30:24,255
Mr. Bowen. I'm Charles Gordon,
your stage manager.

454
00:30:24,279 --> 00:30:25,256
Oh, it's nice to see you.

455
00:30:25,280 --> 00:30:27,158
Everything's ready, sir.

456
00:30:27,182 --> 00:30:30,328
Oh, good. See that girl in
the green dress near the end,

457
00:30:30,352 --> 00:30:31,095
changing her shoes?

458
00:30:31,119 --> 00:30:33,097
Yes, sir.

459
00:30:33,121 --> 00:30:35,233
Let's begin with her.
They can each show me a few steps.

460
00:30:35,257 --> 00:30:37,086
Very good, sir.

461
00:30:40,795 --> 00:30:42,506
Your name, please?

462
00:30:42,530 --> 00:30:44,008
Anne Ashmond.

463
00:30:44,032 --> 00:30:46,177
Thank you. Will you begin, please?
Why, yes.

464
00:30:46,201 --> 00:30:47,897
Miss Anne Ashmond.

465
00:31:25,140 --> 00:31:27,318
Will you try a few steps
with me, please?

466
00:31:27,342 --> 00:31:28,552
Oh, dear.

467
00:31:28,576 --> 00:31:30,187
Can't you dance with a partner?

468
00:31:30,211 --> 00:31:32,023
Well, I could till a moment ago.

469
00:31:32,047 --> 00:31:33,791
Well, try.
We'll take something easy to start.

470
00:31:33,815 --> 00:31:35,445
May I have a pickup, please?

471
00:31:44,492 --> 00:31:47,138
You should've seen the expression
on your face when you saw me.

472
00:31:47,162 --> 00:31:48,172
How did I look?

473
00:31:48,196 --> 00:31:49,826
As if I were a dentist.

474
00:31:56,871 --> 00:31:58,983
You dance very well.
Thank you.

475
00:31:59,007 --> 00:32:00,751
I think you'll do fine.
Will I really?

476
00:32:00,775 --> 00:32:02,405
Sure.

477
00:32:05,280 --> 00:32:08,526
It's very nice of you not to
hold what happened against me.

478
00:32:08,550 --> 00:32:10,661
I do a little bit.
Well, what can I do?

479
00:32:10,685 --> 00:32:12,363
You can have dinner
with me some night.

480
00:32:12,387 --> 00:32:13,497
Well, I should love to.

481
00:32:13,521 --> 00:32:14,598
How about tonight?

482
00:32:14,622 --> 00:32:16,767
Well, I don't know.
I, uh...

483
00:32:16,791 --> 00:32:20,823
she'll do fine. Pick you up at 8:00.
Thank you. Who's next?

484
00:32:27,736 --> 00:32:30,348
Is it much further?
No. We're almost there.

485
00:32:30,372 --> 00:32:32,583
My, you look pretty.

486
00:32:32,607 --> 00:32:35,119
I know. What do you
have to do at the house?

487
00:32:35,143 --> 00:32:37,922
Get the wedding present.
We sold practically everything at auction,

488
00:32:37,946 --> 00:32:39,623
except for one set of China plates.

489
00:32:39,647 --> 00:32:42,393
I was supposed to get those
when I got married.

490
00:32:42,417 --> 00:32:44,128
Seeing there's precious
little chance of that,

491
00:32:44,152 --> 00:32:45,863
we decided to hand them on
to the prince and Princess.

492
00:32:45,887 --> 00:32:47,798
Why is there precious little chance?

493
00:32:47,822 --> 00:32:50,134
Oh, I don't know.

494
00:32:50,158 --> 00:32:51,535
I think you have to enjoy
living with yourself

495
00:32:51,559 --> 00:32:53,437
before you have nerve
to ask anyone else to.

496
00:32:53,461 --> 00:32:57,441
Besides, you know how I am.

497
00:32:57,465 --> 00:32:58,761
Oh, here we are.

498
00:33:05,573 --> 00:33:07,218
John, it's beautiful.

499
00:33:07,242 --> 00:33:09,739
Yes, it was.

500
00:33:17,285 --> 00:33:20,264
I think they're in here.

501
00:33:20,288 --> 00:33:21,399
Oh, what a wonderful
floor to dance on.

502
00:33:21,423 --> 00:33:22,967
Yes.

503
00:33:22,991 --> 00:33:24,769
How long since anyone's
lived here, John?

504
00:33:24,793 --> 00:33:26,404
Oh, about 5 years.

505
00:33:26,428 --> 00:33:28,406
No one can afford
places like this today.

506
00:33:28,430 --> 00:33:30,474
I imagine you miss it terribly.

507
00:33:30,498 --> 00:33:33,077
No, I don't think I do anymore.

508
00:33:33,101 --> 00:33:35,646
I just don't have anything
to replace it with.

509
00:33:35,670 --> 00:33:37,248
I say, what time
do you have to be back?

510
00:33:37,272 --> 00:33:38,749
7:00 sharp.

511
00:33:38,773 --> 00:33:40,751
Oh, I wish you could
have dinner with me.

512
00:33:40,775 --> 00:33:42,486
I don't know when I'll
get another free evening.

513
00:33:42,510 --> 00:33:45,423
There's some sort of party
every night from now on.

514
00:33:45,447 --> 00:33:46,624
Can't you?

515
00:33:46,648 --> 00:33:49,527
I don't know.
You know how Tom is.

516
00:33:49,551 --> 00:33:50,961
Will you try?

517
00:33:50,985 --> 00:33:52,129
Yes, I'll try.

518
00:33:52,153 --> 00:33:53,649
Good.

519
00:33:55,190 --> 00:33:56,934
Hello.

520
00:33:56,958 --> 00:33:58,936
Hi. Back on time.

521
00:33:58,960 --> 00:34:00,337
How was the afternoon?

522
00:34:00,361 --> 00:34:01,939
Oh, it was wonderful.
How were your dancers?

523
00:34:01,963 --> 00:34:03,307
Dancers.

524
00:34:03,331 --> 00:34:04,975
What do you want to do tonight?

525
00:34:04,999 --> 00:34:07,445
Oh, I'm kind of tired, Ellie.

526
00:34:07,469 --> 00:34:10,047
I thought I'd just skip
dinner and go straight to bed.

527
00:34:10,071 --> 00:34:11,449
Would you mind?

528
00:34:11,473 --> 00:34:13,651
Oh, no, no.
It's a good idea.

529
00:34:13,675 --> 00:34:15,186
That country air
really knocks me out.

530
00:34:15,210 --> 00:34:16,854
Yeah.
Sure you don't mind?

531
00:34:16,878 --> 00:34:19,290
Oh, gosh, no.
As a matter of fact,

532
00:34:19,314 --> 00:34:21,325
I think I'll turn in right now,
if it's all right with you?

533
00:34:21,349 --> 00:34:22,960
It's a good idea.
Yeah.

534
00:34:22,984 --> 00:34:24,995
It's been a kind of a rough day.
Yeah.

535
00:34:25,019 --> 00:34:26,197
Good night, honey.

536
00:34:26,221 --> 00:34:29,518
Good night, Tommy.
Sweet dreams.

537
00:35:01,422 --> 00:35:03,819
Taxi, please.

538
00:35:05,860 --> 00:35:07,690
Thank you.

539
00:35:09,063 --> 00:35:10,860
Taxi.

540
00:35:14,202 --> 00:35:15,831
Thank you.

541
00:35:32,487 --> 00:35:34,632
More coffee?
Thank you.

542
00:35:34,656 --> 00:35:36,934
Yes, Ellie and I have done
quite a few shows together.

543
00:35:36,958 --> 00:35:38,702
Do you like to dance?

544
00:35:38,726 --> 00:35:41,605
Yes, yes.
It's hard work, but it's fun.

545
00:35:41,629 --> 00:35:44,041
What made you decide to dance?

546
00:35:44,065 --> 00:35:45,709
Oh, a very silly reason.

547
00:35:45,733 --> 00:35:47,278
How silly?

548
00:35:47,302 --> 00:35:50,481
Well, when I was 11,
I fell in love for the first time

549
00:35:50,505 --> 00:35:52,049
with a boy much older.

550
00:35:52,073 --> 00:35:53,117
12?

551
00:35:53,141 --> 00:35:54,785
Mm-mmm. 13.

552
00:35:54,809 --> 00:35:58,055
His name was Alonzo,
and I was so happy

553
00:35:58,079 --> 00:36:00,291
that suddenly all I
wanted to do was to dance,

554
00:36:00,315 --> 00:36:03,627
so I figured that if I
danced when I was happy,

555
00:36:03,651 --> 00:36:05,262
I should be happy if I danced.

556
00:36:05,286 --> 00:36:06,664
Is that silly enough?

557
00:36:06,688 --> 00:36:08,465
I think so.

558
00:36:08,489 --> 00:36:10,668
I felt so good about Alonzo

559
00:36:10,692 --> 00:36:13,771
I used to close my eyes and pretend
I could dance all over the floor,

560
00:36:13,795 --> 00:36:15,406
walls, even the ceiling.

561
00:36:15,430 --> 00:36:16,774
If you ever learn to do that,

562
00:36:16,798 --> 00:36:18,442
I can get you a very good booking.

563
00:36:18,466 --> 00:36:19,476
You want anything else?

564
00:36:19,500 --> 00:36:21,664
No.
Let's go.

565
00:36:25,940 --> 00:36:27,451
You better not take me home.

566
00:36:27,475 --> 00:36:29,019
Why not?

567
00:36:29,043 --> 00:36:30,921
It's a long way, and
besides, this is Friday.

568
00:36:30,945 --> 00:36:32,690
And Friday I have to stop
and see my father.

569
00:36:32,714 --> 00:36:34,458
Oh, I'd like to meet him. Cabby!

570
00:36:34,482 --> 00:36:35,526
All right. Uh, what's the address?

571
00:36:35,550 --> 00:36:37,328
150 Mitchell street.

572
00:36:37,352 --> 00:36:39,296
Cabby, could you take us
to 150 Mitchell street?

573
00:36:39,320 --> 00:36:42,017
Love to, governor.
Love to.

574
00:36:49,163 --> 00:36:50,474
Wait, will you please, driver?

575
00:36:50,498 --> 00:36:51,976
Love to, governor, love to.

576
00:36:52,000 --> 00:36:53,978
See? That's my father.
He's the proprietor.

577
00:36:55,370 --> 00:36:57,348
Oh, dear. I do hope
he won't offend you.

578
00:36:57,372 --> 00:36:58,949
He's quite impossible, really.

579
00:36:58,973 --> 00:37:00,551
He and my mum have been
separated for 3 years,

580
00:37:00,575 --> 00:37:03,187
and I have to stop here every
Friday and get her money.

581
00:37:03,211 --> 00:37:04,688
Why doesn't your mother do it?

582
00:37:04,712 --> 00:37:05,889
They're not speaking to each other.

583
00:37:05,913 --> 00:37:07,157
So I says to him

584
00:37:07,181 --> 00:37:08,792
"McBride, every time you get 4 drinks

585
00:37:08,816 --> 00:37:10,194
"under that belt of yours,

586
00:37:10,218 --> 00:37:11,996
"you become a bloomin' nuisance.

587
00:37:12,020 --> 00:37:13,998
"And what's more,
every time you fall down,

588
00:37:14,022 --> 00:37:16,200
you chip a piece out of
the bar with your chin."

589
00:37:16,224 --> 00:37:17,801
So I says,
"from now on..."

590
00:37:17,825 --> 00:37:20,404
Annie! Well, my little
girl, how are you, dear?

591
00:37:20,428 --> 00:37:22,206
Hello, Jamie. Tom, this is my father.

592
00:37:22,230 --> 00:37:23,641
Father, this is Tom Bowen.

593
00:37:23,665 --> 00:37:24,808
Glad to know you,
Mr. Ashmond.

594
00:37:24,832 --> 00:37:25,809
Pleased to meet you, sir.

595
00:37:25,833 --> 00:37:27,411
To the royal couple.

596
00:37:27,435 --> 00:37:30,014
Bowen, eh?
I used to know a Willy Bowen.

597
00:37:30,038 --> 00:37:31,815
Good old Willy.
Married a girl

598
00:37:31,839 --> 00:37:34,418
we used to know named Gladys Oxley.

599
00:37:34,442 --> 00:37:36,654
I don't think you knew Gladys, Annie.

600
00:37:36,678 --> 00:37:38,222
Very happy they were for years.

601
00:37:38,246 --> 00:37:39,823
Then one night, good old Willy

602
00:37:39,847 --> 00:37:42,660
threw her right out of the window.
Nobody knows why.

603
00:37:42,684 --> 00:37:45,429
But I always figured that
they must've had an argument.

604
00:37:45,453 --> 00:37:47,665
I don't suppose you'd
be any relation.

605
00:37:47,689 --> 00:37:49,833
Why, no. I don't believe I
have any relatives over here.

606
00:37:49,857 --> 00:37:51,568
Over here?
I'm from America.

607
00:37:51,592 --> 00:37:53,837
America?! Get out of my pub!
Leave the premises!

608
00:37:53,861 --> 00:37:55,673
I'm not on speaking terms
with the United States!

609
00:37:55,697 --> 00:37:57,041
Jamie! How dare you bring

610
00:37:57,065 --> 00:37:57,941
another yank to my tavern!

611
00:37:57,965 --> 00:37:59,843
Out, do you hear?
Out!

612
00:37:59,867 --> 00:38:00,911
Wait. What's the matter
with the United States?

613
00:38:00,935 --> 00:38:03,347
Matter? You owe me
money, you do.

614
00:38:03,371 --> 00:38:05,449
Where is it?
Here, look at this.

615
00:38:05,473 --> 00:38:07,718
£2.10 run up by
your bloomin' soldiers,

616
00:38:07,742 --> 00:38:09,353
and what do they do?
Upped it!

617
00:38:09,377 --> 00:38:10,554
Walked out without payin'!

618
00:38:10,578 --> 00:38:11,555
I'll not serve you a drink, sir.

619
00:38:11,579 --> 00:38:12,923
Now stop it, Jamie.

620
00:38:12,947 --> 00:38:14,725
To the royal couple.

621
00:38:14,749 --> 00:38:16,727
Well, I can't allow
Anglo-American relations

622
00:38:16,751 --> 00:38:18,362
to be threatened like this.

623
00:38:18,386 --> 00:38:19,563
I'd like to square that bill.

624
00:38:19,587 --> 00:38:20,931
Tom, you shouldn't.

625
00:38:20,955 --> 00:38:23,367
Oh, sure. How much
is that? £2.10?

626
00:38:23,391 --> 00:38:24,735
There you are, sir.

627
00:38:24,759 --> 00:38:26,570
Now there's a gentleman for you.

628
00:38:26,594 --> 00:38:28,739
Not at all like that other
yank you're so fond of.

629
00:38:28,763 --> 00:38:30,441
Yes. Now,
you can just add 10 Bob to that

630
00:38:30,465 --> 00:38:31,942
and give it to me for mother.

631
00:38:31,966 --> 00:38:33,944
Has she found out when
we go to the palace yet?

632
00:38:33,968 --> 00:38:35,479
Or is she keepin' it from me?

633
00:38:35,503 --> 00:38:36,814
£3.00, Jamie.

634
00:38:36,838 --> 00:38:38,749
You see, we sent a little token

635
00:38:38,773 --> 00:38:40,751
to the royal highnesses
for the weddin',

636
00:38:40,775 --> 00:38:42,753
and accordin' to the
papers, anybody who did

637
00:38:42,777 --> 00:38:44,722
are allowed to see
the weddin' presents.

638
00:38:44,746 --> 00:38:46,423
That's a nice custom.

639
00:38:46,447 --> 00:38:48,559
£3.00, Jamie.

640
00:38:48,583 --> 00:38:50,613
Oh, all right. Here.

641
00:38:52,520 --> 00:38:54,898
What you countin' for?
Don't you trust me?

642
00:38:54,922 --> 00:38:56,800
Just like her mother...
no faith.

643
00:38:56,824 --> 00:38:58,502
You're 5 shillings short.

644
00:38:58,526 --> 00:39:01,505
Oh? That's what you might
call a typographical error.

645
00:39:01,529 --> 00:39:03,240
Here.
Thank you.

646
00:39:03,264 --> 00:39:05,309
We'd better go.
Good night, Jamie.

647
00:39:05,333 --> 00:39:07,111
Good night, dearie.
Good night, pal.

648
00:39:07,135 --> 00:39:08,112
Good night, buddy.

649
00:39:08,136 --> 00:39:10,332
To the royal couple.

650
00:39:19,347 --> 00:39:20,524
Thanks.

651
00:39:20,548 --> 00:39:22,126
Looks like mother is still up.

652
00:39:22,150 --> 00:39:23,927
Ever since she and Jamie separated,

653
00:39:23,951 --> 00:39:26,130
she can never get to
sleep till I get home.

654
00:39:26,154 --> 00:39:29,151
Let's walk a minute
and get some nice fresh fog.

655
00:39:38,366 --> 00:39:41,345
Who, uh...
who's the yank you're so fond of?

656
00:39:41,369 --> 00:39:42,780
Hal Rayton.

657
00:39:42,804 --> 00:39:45,015
He's my fiancé.

658
00:39:45,039 --> 00:39:46,784
You don't sound very happy about it.

659
00:39:46,808 --> 00:39:48,152
Oh, I am, really.

660
00:39:48,176 --> 00:39:49,553
Is it a secret?

661
00:39:49,577 --> 00:39:51,789
No. Well, this
is ridiculous.

662
00:39:51,813 --> 00:39:53,157
He's in Chicago.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chicago}

663
00:39:53,181 --> 00:39:54,158
Illinois?

664
00:39:54,182 --> 00:39:55,159
He lives there.

665
00:39:55,183 --> 00:39:56,160
And you live here?

666
00:39:56,184 --> 00:39:57,561
Yes.

667
00:39:57,585 --> 00:39:59,062
If you keep that arrangement
after your marriage,

668
00:39:59,086 --> 00:40:01,165
you'll be very happy.

669
00:40:01,189 --> 00:40:02,533
How long since you've seen him?

670
00:40:02,557 --> 00:40:03,834
Oh, about 2 years.

671
00:40:03,858 --> 00:40:05,702
2 years?

672
00:40:05,726 --> 00:40:07,571
And you're still in love with him?

673
00:40:07,595 --> 00:40:09,573
Well, I've never
thought of not being.

674
00:40:09,597 --> 00:40:11,141
Do you hear from him very often?

675
00:40:11,165 --> 00:40:12,643
Every few days, as a rule.

676
00:40:14,602 --> 00:40:16,980
Actually, I haven't heard
from him for 2 months now,

677
00:40:17,004 --> 00:40:18,816
but he's probably been busy.

678
00:40:18,840 --> 00:40:20,050
I wrote to him tonight

679
00:40:20,074 --> 00:40:21,685
and told him I was in your show

680
00:40:21,709 --> 00:40:23,821
and asked him to call
me opening night.

681
00:40:23,845 --> 00:40:26,223
I assume you have some plans about
getting together in the future.

682
00:40:26,247 --> 00:40:27,825
Oh, yes.

683
00:40:27,849 --> 00:40:29,993
Are you going over there,
is he coming over here,

684
00:40:30,017 --> 00:40:32,596
or are you going
to meet in the middle?

685
00:40:32,620 --> 00:40:35,399
You see, he works in Ogilvy's
department store in Chicago.

686
00:40:35,423 --> 00:40:37,000
Oh? Well?

687
00:40:37,024 --> 00:40:39,203
As soon as he makes
enough to send for me...

688
00:40:39,227 --> 00:40:41,004
Or I make enough to go over there,

689
00:40:41,028 --> 00:40:42,239
we're going to be married.

690
00:40:42,263 --> 00:40:44,026
That's fine.

691
00:40:46,033 --> 00:40:47,845
You... you don't
mind, do you?

692
00:40:47,869 --> 00:40:49,012
Mind what?

693
00:40:49,036 --> 00:40:50,614
My getting married, I mean.

694
00:40:50,638 --> 00:40:52,249
I should love to go
out with you again.

695
00:40:52,273 --> 00:40:53,684
I had an awfully good time.

696
00:40:53,708 --> 00:40:55,853
So did I. Of course, I don't mind.

697
00:40:55,877 --> 00:40:57,855
Actually, I'm kind of glad
you're all tied up.

698
00:40:57,879 --> 00:40:59,223
Now we can go out and have fun

699
00:40:59,247 --> 00:41:00,624
without any pressure, can't we?

700
00:41:00,648 --> 00:41:02,878
Yes...
I guess so.

701
00:41:03,651 --> 00:41:05,028
Good night, Anne.

702
00:41:05,052 --> 00:41:06,682
Good night, Tom.

703
00:41:18,132 --> 00:41:20,544
Yes, this is New York.
We have him on the line.

704
00:41:20,568 --> 00:41:22,379
Mr. Irving Klinger,

705
00:41:22,403 --> 00:41:24,248
we're ready with Mr. Edgar
Klinger in London.

706
00:41:24,272 --> 00:41:25,449
Go ahead, please.

707
00:41:25,473 --> 00:41:26,817
Hello, ed?
This is Irv!

708
00:41:26,841 --> 00:41:28,218
How are you, twinsey?

709
00:41:28,242 --> 00:41:30,220
Buzzing, old boy.
Simply buzzing.

710
00:41:30,244 --> 00:41:32,623
I got a fast note by air
mail from Tombo this morning.

711
00:41:32,647 --> 00:41:33,991
He says everything is terrif.

712
00:41:34,015 --> 00:41:35,993
Oh, well, that is good news.

713
00:41:36,017 --> 00:41:38,996
Tell me, have you heard from Tom?

714
00:41:39,020 --> 00:41:40,831
No. How are things?

715
00:41:40,855 --> 00:41:42,232
Oh, absolutely superb.

716
00:41:42,256 --> 00:41:43,834
I think we shall have

717
00:41:43,858 --> 00:41:46,003
a fantastic opening
night tomorrow night.

718
00:41:46,027 --> 00:41:47,437
What about Ellen?
Tom having trouble

719
00:41:47,461 --> 00:41:49,006
keeping her caged up at night?

720
00:41:49,030 --> 00:41:50,641
Oh, no. Quite
the contrary.

721
00:41:50,665 --> 00:41:52,476
She's been frightfully conscientious.

722
00:41:52,500 --> 00:41:54,645
Goes straight home from
the theater each night.

723
00:41:54,669 --> 00:41:56,413
Tom's the one who's
been romping about.

724
00:41:56,437 --> 00:41:57,648
Tom? No kidding?

725
00:41:57,672 --> 00:41:59,249
What'd he do, catch himself a chick?

726
00:41:59,273 --> 00:42:01,652
No, no, no.
Tom's quite well.

727
00:42:01,676 --> 00:42:04,254
He's taken a fancy to
some girl in the show.

728
00:42:04,278 --> 00:42:05,956
Pretty little thing she is, too.

729
00:42:05,980 --> 00:42:07,257
Hey, Eddie, what
I called you about...

730
00:42:07,281 --> 00:42:09,159
call me after the opening, will you?

731
00:42:09,183 --> 00:42:11,662
That light in the window's
gonna be me sitting and waiting.

732
00:42:11,686 --> 00:42:14,031
I will, old boy.
Don't give it a second thought.

733
00:42:14,055 --> 00:42:15,766
Oh, by the by, how's the mater?

734
00:42:15,790 --> 00:42:17,668
Oh, mom's fine.

735
00:42:17,692 --> 00:42:20,037
Oh, splendid.
Do give her a peck on the cheek for me.

736
00:42:20,061 --> 00:42:21,371
I got you.
Dig you tomorrow night.

737
00:42:21,395 --> 00:42:23,040
Pip now.

738
00:42:23,064 --> 00:42:24,293
"Pip now"?

739
00:42:25,499 --> 00:42:26,929
"Dig you"?

740
00:42:31,806 --> 00:42:33,884
What's all the commotion
about down there?

741
00:42:33,908 --> 00:42:36,286
It's some regiment that hasn't
paraded its finery in years.

742
00:42:36,310 --> 00:42:38,555
Gosh, you can just feel the
excitement growing, can't you?

743
00:42:38,579 --> 00:42:40,958
After all, the wedding's
only a week off.

744
00:42:40,982 --> 00:42:43,226
I wonder what the groom
is doing this morning.

745
00:42:43,250 --> 00:42:45,028
Why don't you call him and ask him?

746
00:42:45,052 --> 00:42:46,029
Very funny.

747
00:42:46,053 --> 00:42:47,965
I thought it was.

748
00:42:47,989 --> 00:42:50,233
What time did you get in last
night, lover boy?

749
00:42:50,257 --> 00:42:51,868
Oh, around 11:00,
I guess.

750
00:42:51,892 --> 00:42:53,337
I don't know.
I didn't pay much attention.

751
00:42:53,361 --> 00:42:55,672
Well, I did.
It was around 2:00.

752
00:42:55,696 --> 00:42:58,241
My, you're a busy little man these
P.M.S, aren't you?

753
00:42:58,265 --> 00:42:59,876
We were just having a few laughs.

754
00:42:59,900 --> 00:43:00,877
Just a few laughs.

755
00:43:00,901 --> 00:43:01,901
That's all.

756
00:43:04,071 --> 00:43:05,515
Come in.

757
00:43:05,539 --> 00:43:07,084
Morning.

758
00:43:07,108 --> 00:43:08,518
Why, Johnny,
what a pleasant surprise.

759
00:43:08,542 --> 00:43:09,419
How are you?
Fine.

760
00:43:09,443 --> 00:43:10,754
Hi, John.
Hello, Tom.

761
00:43:10,778 --> 00:43:11,888
I thought this was as
good a place as any

762
00:43:11,912 --> 00:43:13,156
to see the parade.

763
00:43:13,180 --> 00:43:14,691
Oh, we have a sensational view.

764
00:43:14,715 --> 00:43:16,026
Oh, before I forget...
your ticket for the opening.

765
00:43:16,050 --> 00:43:17,227
Second row, right on the aisle.

766
00:43:17,251 --> 00:43:18,228
Wonderful.
Thanks very much.

767
00:43:18,252 --> 00:43:19,496
What time is the parade?

768
00:43:19,520 --> 00:43:21,064
It should pass here any minute.

769
00:43:21,088 --> 00:43:22,432
I say, Ellen,
wonderful seeing you again.

770
00:43:22,456 --> 00:43:23,767
How's the show going?

771
00:43:23,791 --> 00:43:26,403
Oh, just fine.
It's been a long time.

772
00:43:26,427 --> 00:43:27,838
I know. 4 days.

773
00:43:27,862 --> 00:43:29,406
Look, tomorrow night
after the opening,

774
00:43:29,430 --> 00:43:30,540
Edgar's giving a party.

775
00:43:30,564 --> 00:43:32,209
You're going with me, OK?

776
00:43:32,233 --> 00:43:33,243
OK. It'll take a little
doing, though.

777
00:43:33,267 --> 00:43:34,845
Why?

778
00:43:34,869 --> 00:43:36,179
There's a huge affair being given,

779
00:43:36,203 --> 00:43:37,447
and I'm supposed to take someone.

780
00:43:37,471 --> 00:43:38,515
But you can get out of it, can't you?

781
00:43:38,539 --> 00:43:40,017
This is my opening night.

782
00:43:40,041 --> 00:43:42,019
Besides, if you don't
take me, nobody will.

783
00:43:42,043 --> 00:43:44,521
I haven't been out with a
soul but you since I got here.

784
00:43:44,545 --> 00:43:45,941
I'll get out of it.

785
00:43:47,448 --> 00:43:48,625
You're looking so well these days.

786
00:43:48,649 --> 00:43:49,860
Thank you.

787
00:43:49,884 --> 00:43:51,228
I think about you so much.

788
00:43:51,252 --> 00:43:52,396
I think about you, Ellen. What?

789
00:43:52,420 --> 00:43:54,431
I said, I think about you.

790
00:43:54,455 --> 00:43:56,199
It's so different from
anything I've ever felt before.

791
00:43:56,223 --> 00:43:57,634
How?

792
00:43:57,658 --> 00:43:59,636
Well, first of all,
I'm not interested

793
00:43:59,660 --> 00:44:01,438
in anybody or anything I do,

794
00:44:01,462 --> 00:44:02,873
and that's completely new for me.

795
00:44:04,565 --> 00:44:07,644
I say, that's completely new for me.

796
00:44:07,668 --> 00:44:11,266
Ellen, I think that this
is getting very serious.

797
00:44:15,976 --> 00:44:17,487
What a parade.

798
00:44:17,511 --> 00:44:19,322
I've never seen anything
like it in my life.

799
00:44:19,346 --> 00:44:20,157
Parade?

800
00:44:20,181 --> 00:44:21,324
Oh, is it over?

801
00:44:21,348 --> 00:44:23,126
I'll tell them to come back.

802
00:44:23,150 --> 00:44:25,395
Ignore him, Johnny.
I'm so excited about tomorrow night.

803
00:44:25,419 --> 00:44:26,763
Come on, girl.

804
00:44:26,787 --> 00:44:28,131
We're due at the
theater 5 minutes ago.

805
00:44:28,155 --> 00:44:29,800
In a minute.

806
00:44:29,824 --> 00:44:31,334
Do you realize we've only
been out at night together

807
00:44:31,358 --> 00:44:33,136
about 3 times since I've been here?

808
00:44:33,160 --> 00:44:34,137
Well... come on, come on.

809
00:44:34,161 --> 00:44:36,425
Just a minute.

810
00:44:38,032 --> 00:44:40,143
What's that you're playing, Tom?

811
00:44:40,167 --> 00:44:43,346
The song she's supposed to be
rehearsing at the theater now.

812
00:44:43,370 --> 00:44:45,348
Well, why don't you rehearse it here?

813
00:44:45,372 --> 00:44:47,350
Oh, that's a good idea.

814
00:44:47,374 --> 00:44:49,352
Is that all right with you, Tommy?

815
00:44:49,376 --> 00:44:50,506
All right.

816
00:44:52,646 --> 00:44:58,628
♪ I wake up and sigh
each morning ♪

817
00:44:58,652 --> 00:45:04,201
♪ happy the night's
gone by ♪

818
00:45:04,225 --> 00:45:11,041
♪ I wake up and pray
each morning ♪

819
00:45:11,065 --> 00:45:17,447
♪ pray that the day
will fly ♪

820
00:45:17,471 --> 00:45:25,222
♪ and then I
sit back and smile ♪

821
00:45:25,246 --> 00:45:30,227
♪ and dream of that day ♪

822
00:45:30,251 --> 00:45:38,251
♪ when I'll be standing
by your side, my love ♪

823
00:45:40,261 --> 00:45:47,496
♪ the happiest day
of my life ♪

824
00:45:50,104 --> 00:45:58,104
♪ how my heart will swell
with pride, my love ♪

825
00:45:59,280 --> 00:46:07,280
♪ the happiest day
in a lifetime ♪

826
00:46:09,290 --> 00:46:17,290
♪ then as the last words
are spoken ♪

827
00:46:18,532 --> 00:46:26,532
♪ the bells in the steeple
will chime ♪

828
00:46:30,811 --> 00:46:38,628
♪ and I will love you so ♪

829
00:46:38,652 --> 00:46:45,135
♪ and you will see ♪

830
00:46:45,159 --> 00:46:49,739
♪ it will be ♪

831
00:46:49,763 --> 00:46:57,532
♪ for a lifetime ♪

832
00:47:18,325 --> 00:47:20,303
Hold it. Hold it, please.

833
00:47:20,327 --> 00:47:22,305
Charlie, what time is it?

834
00:47:22,329 --> 00:47:23,607
Uh, it's 4 A.M., sir.

835
00:47:23,631 --> 00:47:25,475
Well, that's enough.

836
00:47:25,499 --> 00:47:27,344
All right, everybody.
That'll be all for tonight.

837
00:47:27,368 --> 00:47:28,812
I'm sorry to keep you
working so late.

838
00:47:28,836 --> 00:47:30,247
What about our last 2 numbers?

839
00:47:30,271 --> 00:47:31,815
We don't have to rehearse those.

840
00:47:31,839 --> 00:47:32,782
You were fine on them this afternoon.

841
00:47:32,806 --> 00:47:34,017
Yeah, I thought so, too.

842
00:47:34,041 --> 00:47:35,418
Show looks pretty good, doesn't it?

843
00:47:35,442 --> 00:47:36,419
Not bad.
Anne?

844
00:47:36,443 --> 00:47:38,421
Yes?
Excuse me.

845
00:47:38,445 --> 00:47:40,023
Don't forget tomorrow night.

846
00:47:40,047 --> 00:47:41,825
The opening?
How could I?

847
00:47:41,849 --> 00:47:43,093
Oh, yes, do try to make that.

848
00:47:43,117 --> 00:47:44,628
We'd love to have you.

849
00:47:44,652 --> 00:47:47,030
No, I meant afterward.
Edgar's giving a party.

850
00:47:47,054 --> 00:47:48,031
We'll go together.

851
00:47:48,055 --> 00:47:50,267
Oh, Tom, I can't.

852
00:47:50,291 --> 00:47:51,768
You can't?
Why not?

853
00:47:51,792 --> 00:47:53,436
Hal is calling me.

854
00:47:53,460 --> 00:47:56,273
That's right.
I forgot all about him.

855
00:47:56,297 --> 00:47:57,307
Well, it doesn't matter.

856
00:47:57,331 --> 00:47:59,342
I wouldn't bother,

857
00:47:59,366 --> 00:48:01,278
except I haven't heard from
him in such a long time now,

858
00:48:01,302 --> 00:48:02,812
and I do have to talk to him.

859
00:48:02,836 --> 00:48:04,481
Taking the bus home tonight, Anne?

860
00:48:04,505 --> 00:48:05,882
I'll be right with you.

861
00:48:05,906 --> 00:48:07,284
I'm sorry, Tom.

862
00:48:07,308 --> 00:48:10,487
That's all right.
It's all right.

863
00:48:10,511 --> 00:48:13,957
What time tomorrow, sir?

864
00:48:13,981 --> 00:48:16,226
3:00 for the company.
11:00 for miss Bowen and me.

865
00:48:16,250 --> 00:48:17,227
Yes, sir.

866
00:48:17,251 --> 00:48:19,129
11:00?
What for, Tom?

867
00:48:19,153 --> 00:48:21,331
I wanna take those 2 numbers
we didn't do tonight.

868
00:48:21,355 --> 00:48:23,166
I thought you were
satisfied with them.

869
00:48:23,190 --> 00:48:24,901
There's still a few rough spots.

870
00:48:24,925 --> 00:48:27,504
Say, what happened to you?
Did you get stood up?

871
00:48:27,528 --> 00:48:29,306
What are you talking about?

872
00:48:29,330 --> 00:48:31,908
My, what a manic-depressive
life you lead.

873
00:48:31,932 --> 00:48:34,511
It's going to be a marvelous
party tomorrow night.

874
00:48:34,535 --> 00:48:37,347
I'm going with John.
Who are you taking?

875
00:48:37,371 --> 00:48:40,984
♪ Every night at 7:00 ♪

876
00:48:41,008 --> 00:48:43,538
♪ dum bum bum Dee um... ♪

877
00:48:48,182 --> 00:48:49,025
Where's the key?

878
00:48:49,049 --> 00:48:50,160
You have it.

879
00:48:50,184 --> 00:48:51,928
I have not.
I gave it to you.

880
00:48:51,952 --> 00:48:54,331
I suppose I'll have to go all
the way down to the desk...

881
00:48:54,355 --> 00:48:56,333
see? If you think
nice things,

882
00:48:56,357 --> 00:49:00,203
all doors open to you.

883
00:49:00,227 --> 00:49:03,406
Is that your message for the day?

884
00:49:03,430 --> 00:49:05,275
Flowers! For me?

885
00:49:05,299 --> 00:49:06,776
I wonder who they're from.

886
00:49:06,800 --> 00:49:08,011
Not from me.

887
00:49:08,035 --> 00:49:09,045
Well, that I know.

888
00:49:09,069 --> 00:49:10,680
Who?

889
00:49:10,704 --> 00:49:13,216
It's from John.
He can't make the opening.

890
00:49:13,240 --> 00:49:15,352
He can't?
My, what a shame.

891
00:49:15,376 --> 00:49:17,854
I had a feeling this morning he
wasn't gonna be able to make it,

892
00:49:17,878 --> 00:49:19,222
but he just didn't
know how to tell me.

893
00:49:19,246 --> 00:49:20,457
Now, isn't that terrible?

894
00:49:20,481 --> 00:49:22,125
Now, isn't that terrible?

895
00:49:22,149 --> 00:49:24,260
He's weak,
and I just hate weak people!

896
00:49:24,284 --> 00:49:25,862
Yes, dear, I know you do.

897
00:49:25,886 --> 00:49:27,630
Up one minute, down the next.

898
00:49:27,654 --> 00:49:30,266
What a manic-depressive
life you lead.

899
00:49:30,290 --> 00:49:34,437
Oh, all right, all right.
So we're even.

900
00:49:34,461 --> 00:49:36,639
Who are you going
with tomorrow night?

901
00:49:36,663 --> 00:49:38,074
I don't know.

902
00:49:38,098 --> 00:49:40,710
Well, why don't you take me?

903
00:49:40,734 --> 00:49:42,946
I think we should go together
anyway, don't you?

904
00:49:42,970 --> 00:49:45,281
After all,
we're the stars of the show.

905
00:49:45,305 --> 00:49:46,883
Yes, I think we should.

906
00:49:46,907 --> 00:49:48,818
Miss Bowen, may I escort you

907
00:49:48,842 --> 00:49:50,887
to Klinger's clambake tomorrow night?

908
00:49:50,911 --> 00:49:53,590
Why, I'd be delighted!

909
00:49:53,614 --> 00:49:57,093
And what a surprise you're askin' me!

910
00:49:57,117 --> 00:49:59,396
Oh, Tommy,
let's be terrific tomorrow night.

911
00:49:59,420 --> 00:50:00,397
We'll be cosmic.

912
00:50:00,421 --> 00:50:01,398
Stupendous!

913
00:50:01,422 --> 00:50:03,785
A smash! We hope.

914
00:50:05,192 --> 00:50:06,970
And, Ellen, don't forget...

915
00:50:06,994 --> 00:50:09,406
that's still the
most important thing.

916
00:50:09,430 --> 00:50:12,227
Yes, Tommy...
I know it is.

917
00:50:13,200 --> 00:50:14,177
Good night.

918
00:50:14,201 --> 00:50:15,597
Good night.

919
00:50:27,815 --> 00:50:29,125
Oh, yeah, yeah!

920
00:50:29,149 --> 00:50:30,794
You listen to me just once!

921
00:50:30,818 --> 00:50:32,796
Oh, sure, sure!

922
00:50:32,820 --> 00:50:34,030
I told you a million times.

923
00:50:34,054 --> 00:50:35,832
You never wanna listen to me.

924
00:50:35,856 --> 00:50:37,834
So I said it, so you heard
it, so you're mad.

925
00:50:37,858 --> 00:50:39,002
So what?

926
00:50:39,026 --> 00:50:40,303
So this...
it's the last time

927
00:50:40,327 --> 00:50:41,604
I ever go to a party with you!

928
00:50:41,628 --> 00:50:43,206
Will you put that in writin'?

929
00:50:43,230 --> 00:50:44,441
You're always makin'
cracks, makin' cracks!

930
00:50:44,465 --> 00:50:45,608
Like what?

931
00:50:45,632 --> 00:50:47,444
You always humiliate me, humiliate me.

932
00:50:47,468 --> 00:50:49,212
Didn't your mother never
teach ya no manners?

933
00:50:49,236 --> 00:50:50,180
I ain't never had no mother.

934
00:50:50,204 --> 00:50:51,848
We was too poor.

935
00:50:51,872 --> 00:50:53,216
Say, what's the matter
with you lately?

936
00:50:53,240 --> 00:50:55,018
You used to tell me you loved me.

937
00:50:55,042 --> 00:50:57,454
You used to treat me
like a high-class dame.

938
00:50:57,478 --> 00:50:58,455
Well, usen't ya?

939
00:50:58,479 --> 00:50:59,355
So I used.

940
00:50:59,379 --> 00:51:00,623
There. So you
admit it.

941
00:51:00,647 --> 00:51:01,858
I ain't admittin' nothin'!

942
00:51:01,882 --> 00:51:03,159
I'll give ya one more chance.

943
00:51:03,183 --> 00:51:04,661
Do you love me or don't ya?

944
00:51:04,685 --> 00:51:06,362
No, I don't!

945
00:51:06,386 --> 00:51:09,032
Quit stallin'!
I want a direct answer.

946
00:51:11,258 --> 00:51:14,137
Listen, kid, there's one thing
about you I can't understand...

947
00:51:14,161 --> 00:51:15,705
♪ how could you
believe me ♪

948
00:51:15,729 --> 00:51:17,907
♪ when I said
I loved you ♪

949
00:51:17,931 --> 00:51:22,145
♪ when you know I've been
a liar all my life? ♪

950
00:51:22,169 --> 00:51:25,515
♪ You've had that reputation
since you was a youth ♪

951
00:51:25,539 --> 00:51:26,916
♪ you must've
been insane ♪

952
00:51:26,940 --> 00:51:29,719
♪ to think I'd
tell you the truth ♪

953
00:51:29,743 --> 00:51:31,154
♪ how could
I believe you ♪

954
00:51:31,178 --> 00:51:33,156
♪ when you said
we'd marry? ♪

955
00:51:33,180 --> 00:51:36,926
♪ Why, you know I'd rather
hang than have a wife ♪

956
00:51:36,950 --> 00:51:40,730
♪ I know I said
I'd make you mine ♪

957
00:51:40,754 --> 00:51:42,732
♪ now wouldn't youse
know that I would go ♪

958
00:51:42,756 --> 00:51:44,734
♪ for that
old line ♪

959
00:51:44,758 --> 00:51:46,536
♪ how could you
believe me ♪

960
00:51:46,560 --> 00:51:48,371
♪ when I said
I loved you ♪

961
00:51:48,395 --> 00:51:50,540
♪ when you know
I've been a liar ♪

962
00:51:50,564 --> 00:51:52,375
♪ you sure have
been a liar ♪

963
00:51:52,399 --> 00:51:54,377
♪ a double-crossin'
liar ♪

964
00:51:54,401 --> 00:51:55,945
♪ a double-crossin'
liar ♪

965
00:51:55,969 --> 00:51:59,749
♪ all my doggone
cheatin' life? ♪

966
00:51:59,773 --> 00:52:03,052
♪ You said you would
love me long ♪

967
00:52:03,076 --> 00:52:04,554
So what?

968
00:52:04,578 --> 00:52:07,123
♪ And never would
do me wrong ♪

969
00:52:07,147 --> 00:52:08,558
Stop bending the suit!

970
00:52:08,582 --> 00:52:11,127
♪ Faithful
you'd always be ♪

971
00:52:11,151 --> 00:52:12,395
Me?

972
00:52:12,419 --> 00:52:13,963
♪ Why, baby,
you must be loony ♪

973
00:52:13,987 --> 00:52:18,753
♪ to trust a lower-than-low
two-timer like me ♪

974
00:52:20,594 --> 00:52:23,540
♪ you said I'd
have everything ♪

975
00:52:23,564 --> 00:52:24,974
Get her.

976
00:52:24,998 --> 00:52:27,544
♪ A beautiful
diamond ring ♪

977
00:52:29,236 --> 00:52:31,381
♪ A bungalow
by the sea ♪

978
00:52:31,405 --> 00:52:33,216
A bungalow yet.

979
00:52:33,240 --> 00:52:34,984
♪ You're really naive
to ever believe ♪

980
00:52:35,008 --> 00:52:37,554
♪ a full-of-baloney
phony like me ♪

981
00:52:37,578 --> 00:52:39,556
Boy, I sure must've lost my head.

982
00:52:39,580 --> 00:52:41,591
You ain't lost nothin' you never had.

983
00:52:41,615 --> 00:52:44,594
♪ What about the time
you went to Indiana? ♪

984
00:52:44,618 --> 00:52:45,895
♪ I was lyin' ♪

985
00:52:45,919 --> 00:52:48,665
♪ I was down
in Ala... Bam ♪

986
00:52:48,689 --> 00:52:50,500
♪ you said you
had some business ♪

987
00:52:50,524 --> 00:52:52,502
♪ you had
to complete ♪

988
00:52:52,526 --> 00:52:53,670
♪ what I was doin' ♪

989
00:52:53,694 --> 00:52:56,506
♪ I would be a cad
to repeat ♪

990
00:52:56,530 --> 00:53:00,109
♪ what about the evenin's
you was with your mother? ♪

991
00:53:00,133 --> 00:53:03,613
♪ I was rompin' with
another honey lamb ♪

992
00:53:03,637 --> 00:53:07,917
♪ to think you swore
our love was real ♪

993
00:53:07,941 --> 00:53:11,588
♪ baby, leave us not
forget that I'm a heel ♪

994
00:53:11,612 --> 00:53:14,924
♪ how could I believe you
when you said you loved me? ♪

995
00:53:14,948 --> 00:53:16,693
♪ When you know
I've been a liar ♪

996
00:53:16,717 --> 00:53:18,528
♪ a good-for-nothin'
liar ♪

997
00:53:18,552 --> 00:53:22,517
♪ all my good-for-nothin'
life ♪

998
00:56:16,730 --> 00:56:18,875
♪ You know you've
been a liar ♪

999
00:56:18,899 --> 00:56:20,510
♪ I know I've
been a liar ♪

1000
00:56:20,534 --> 00:56:22,311
♪ a double-crossin'
liar ♪

1001
00:56:22,335 --> 00:56:24,113
♪ a double-crossin'
liar ♪

1002
00:56:24,137 --> 00:56:29,871
♪ all your good-for-nothin' life ♪

1003
00:56:50,330 --> 00:56:51,808
Johnny!

1004
00:56:51,832 --> 00:56:54,210
Darling, I just had to come.
I just had to.

1005
00:56:54,234 --> 00:56:55,645
Who's that with Ellen?
Oh, I'm so glad.

1006
00:56:55,669 --> 00:56:56,679
The chap with the accent.
Who?

1007
00:56:56,703 --> 00:56:57,814
Yes.
Him?

1008
00:56:57,838 --> 00:56:59,415
Ah.
Brindale.

1009
00:56:59,439 --> 00:57:01,384
They've become very good friends.

1010
00:57:01,408 --> 00:57:02,538
Oh, yes.

1011
00:57:04,945 --> 00:57:07,423
Don't you think we should
be getting over to Edgar's?

1012
00:57:07,447 --> 00:57:09,725
Later, Johnny.
Tonight's my night,

1013
00:57:09,749 --> 00:57:11,661
and tonight I want
to be alone with you.

1014
00:57:11,685 --> 00:57:13,529
When you didn't show up
at the opening,

1015
00:57:13,553 --> 00:57:14,897
I didn't even feel like going on.

1016
00:57:14,921 --> 00:57:16,332
How'd you get away from your party?

1017
00:57:16,356 --> 00:57:18,067
Oh, I just walked out.

1018
00:57:18,091 --> 00:57:20,903
I couldn't stand not
being with you tonight.

1019
00:57:20,927 --> 00:57:22,705
Ellen...

1020
00:57:22,729 --> 00:57:24,540
Ellen, I think we're in love.

1021
00:57:24,564 --> 00:57:26,342
Yes, darling.
I know.

1022
00:57:26,366 --> 00:57:28,511
Well, what are we
going to do about it?

1023
00:57:28,535 --> 00:57:30,179
Nothing.

1024
00:57:30,203 --> 00:57:32,548
Well, we can't go on
in this indefinite state.

1025
00:57:32,572 --> 00:57:34,550
Well, aren't you happy?

1026
00:57:34,574 --> 00:57:35,685
Oh, you know I am.

1027
00:57:35,709 --> 00:57:37,053
So am I.

1028
00:57:37,077 --> 00:57:38,940
Let's not kill it
with improvements, huh?

1029
00:57:40,947 --> 00:57:42,792
But... something
might happen.

1030
00:57:42,816 --> 00:57:44,360
Like what?

1031
00:57:44,384 --> 00:57:46,128
Well, someday you might
look over my shoulder

1032
00:57:46,152 --> 00:57:47,563
and see someone else.

1033
00:57:47,587 --> 00:57:48,564
Someone else?

1034
00:57:48,588 --> 00:57:50,184
Yes.

1035
00:57:52,125 --> 00:57:57,273
♪ Too late now
to forget your smile ♪

1036
00:57:57,297 --> 00:58:02,979
♪ the way we cling when
we've danced a while ♪

1037
00:58:03,003 --> 00:58:09,752
♪ too late now to forget
and go on ♪

1038
00:58:09,776 --> 00:58:17,593
♪ to someone new ♪

1039
00:58:17,617 --> 00:58:23,099
♪ too late now
to forget your voice ♪

1040
00:58:23,123 --> 00:58:28,771
♪ the way one word
makes my heart rejoice ♪

1041
00:58:28,795 --> 00:58:36,012
♪ too late now
to imagine myself ♪

1042
00:58:36,036 --> 00:58:43,019
♪ away from you ♪

1043
00:58:43,043 --> 00:58:49,458
♪ all the things
we've done together ♪

1044
00:58:49,482 --> 00:58:54,864
♪ I relive
when we're apart ♪

1045
00:58:54,888 --> 00:59:01,470
♪ all the tender fun
together ♪

1046
00:59:01,494 --> 00:59:09,494
♪ stays on in my heart ♪

1047
00:59:10,070 --> 00:59:16,052
♪ how could I ever
close the door ♪

1048
00:59:16,076 --> 00:59:22,058
♪ and be the same
as I was before? ♪

1049
00:59:22,082 --> 00:59:29,899
♪ Darling, no, no,
I can't anymore ♪

1050
00:59:29,923 --> 00:59:33,502
♪ it's too ♪

1051
00:59:33,526 --> 00:59:38,507
♪ late ♪

1052
00:59:38,531 --> 00:59:43,064
♪ now ♪

1053
01:00:04,090 --> 01:00:05,701
Does Tom know how you feel?

1054
01:00:05,725 --> 01:00:07,269
Oh, goodness, no.

1055
01:00:07,293 --> 01:00:09,071
Do you think he'd mind?

1056
01:00:09,095 --> 01:00:11,273
Well, I imagine if he ever found out,

1057
01:00:11,297 --> 01:00:15,277
he'd get us a booking someplace in South
Africa just to get me away from you.

1058
01:00:15,301 --> 01:00:17,279
Would you forget about
me in South Africa?

1059
01:00:17,303 --> 01:00:18,933
But how could I?

1060
01:00:21,374 --> 01:00:27,123
♪ All the things
we've done together ♪

1061
01:00:27,147 --> 01:00:32,128
♪ I relive
when we're apart ♪

1062
01:00:32,152 --> 01:00:38,768
♪ all the tender fun
together ♪

1063
01:00:38,792 --> 01:00:46,792
♪ stays on in my heart ♪

1064
01:00:48,601 --> 01:00:53,582
♪ how could I ever
close the door ♪

1065
01:00:53,606 --> 01:01:00,589
♪ and be the same
as I was before? ♪

1066
01:01:00,613 --> 01:01:03,592
♪ Darling, no ♪

1067
01:01:03,616 --> 01:01:09,098
♪ no, I can't anymore ♪

1068
01:01:09,122 --> 01:01:16,757
♪ it's too late ♪

1069
01:01:18,431 --> 01:01:22,597
♪ now ♪

1070
01:01:38,718 --> 01:01:39,929
Who is it?

1071
01:01:39,953 --> 01:01:41,315
It's me, Jamie. Tom.

1072
01:01:45,959 --> 01:01:47,703
Tommy, me boy, come in.

1073
01:01:47,727 --> 01:01:48,938
Are you all closed up for the night?

1074
01:01:48,962 --> 01:01:50,506
Well, it's after 2:00.

1075
01:01:50,530 --> 01:01:52,475
But I'd never close
for you, Tommy boy.

1076
01:01:52,499 --> 01:01:53,876
Thanks. The show went well, eh?

1077
01:01:53,900 --> 01:01:55,878
Oh, fine, fine.

1078
01:01:55,902 --> 01:01:57,680
I didn't feel like going home
straight after the party,

1079
01:01:57,704 --> 01:01:58,881
so I thought I'd stop
by and say hello.

1080
01:01:58,905 --> 01:02:00,483
Well, you're just in time.

1081
01:02:00,507 --> 01:02:03,285
It was announced we go to see
the presents on Monday.

1082
01:02:03,309 --> 01:02:06,288
I was just about to try on
me suit for the palace.

1083
01:02:06,312 --> 01:02:08,290
I'll slip into it, you have a look,

1084
01:02:08,314 --> 01:02:09,959
then you can tell me
what you think, eh?

1085
01:02:09,983 --> 01:02:11,160
Sure.

1086
01:02:11,184 --> 01:02:12,695
Oh, uh, by the way,

1087
01:02:12,719 --> 01:02:14,730
isn't this Anne's night to visit you?

1088
01:02:14,754 --> 01:02:16,532
Yeah, she was by about an hour ago,

1089
01:02:16,556 --> 01:02:19,301
picked up her money, and
stole away like a pickpocket.

1090
01:02:19,325 --> 01:02:21,337
Did her call come
through from Chicago?

1091
01:02:21,361 --> 01:02:24,140
No, the bloomin' blighter
never called.

1092
01:02:24,164 --> 01:02:25,074
She must be upset.

1093
01:02:25,098 --> 01:02:26,509
Well, I couldn't say.

1094
01:02:26,533 --> 01:02:29,178
I don't know how Annie
feels about anything.

1095
01:02:29,202 --> 01:02:31,180
She's not an easy one to get to know.

1096
01:02:31,204 --> 01:02:33,015
Very quiet she is, but deep.

1097
01:02:33,039 --> 01:02:34,583
At least I hope she is deep.

1098
01:02:34,607 --> 01:02:37,086
Or else she's wasting
a lot of her time being quiet.

1099
01:02:37,110 --> 01:02:39,822
Well, brace yourself.
What do you think?

1100
01:02:39,846 --> 01:02:41,824
Do I look like a gentleman?

1101
01:02:41,848 --> 01:02:44,026
Jamie, you look like a banker.

1102
01:02:44,050 --> 01:02:45,594
But do I look like a gentleman?

1103
01:02:45,618 --> 01:02:47,196
It's written all over you.

1104
01:02:47,220 --> 01:02:49,598
I got it this afternoon
from Percy Monroe.

1105
01:02:49,622 --> 01:02:51,400
Last year when he got pneumonia,

1106
01:02:51,424 --> 01:02:53,202
they bought it for him
to be buried in.

1107
01:02:53,226 --> 01:02:54,403
But he recovered.

1108
01:02:54,427 --> 01:02:56,605
Well, my only advice is...

1109
01:02:56,629 --> 01:02:59,041
If you meet the royal family...

1110
01:02:59,065 --> 01:03:00,409
I wouldn't bow too low.

1111
01:03:00,433 --> 01:03:01,844
Remember, this is a wedding,

1112
01:03:01,868 --> 01:03:03,397
not a coming-out party.

1113
01:03:04,571 --> 01:03:06,415
I see what you mean.
I'll watch it.

1114
01:03:06,439 --> 01:03:07,416
Are you excited?

1115
01:03:07,440 --> 01:03:08,584
Excited, no.

1116
01:03:08,608 --> 01:03:09,618
I'm just scared.

1117
01:03:09,642 --> 01:03:11,287
Why?
Nervous and scared.

1118
01:03:11,311 --> 01:03:12,855
It's meetin' the old crow again.

1119
01:03:12,879 --> 01:03:14,190
I haven't clapped eyes
on her in 3 years.

1120
01:03:14,214 --> 01:03:16,225
I know.

1121
01:03:16,249 --> 01:03:18,194
It's funny, but if it wasn't
for this royal wedding,

1122
01:03:18,218 --> 01:03:20,429
probably I'd never have
crossed her path again.

1123
01:03:20,453 --> 01:03:22,064
I'll tell you what: On
Monday I'll call for you,

1124
01:03:22,088 --> 01:03:23,232
take you down to meet her.

1125
01:03:23,256 --> 01:03:24,767
I'll give you moral support.

1126
01:03:24,791 --> 01:03:26,702
Now, that's nice of you, Tommy.

1127
01:03:26,726 --> 01:03:28,270
Unusually nice of you.

1128
01:03:28,294 --> 01:03:30,105
You're a good man, you are.

1129
01:03:30,129 --> 01:03:31,473
I don't know what Annie is doing

1130
01:03:31,497 --> 01:03:33,275
waiting for this
knucklehead in Chicago

1131
01:03:33,299 --> 01:03:36,111
when she knows a fella like
you right here in town.

1132
01:03:36,135 --> 01:03:37,913
That's love, Jamie.

1133
01:03:37,937 --> 01:03:39,281
What about you?

1134
01:03:39,305 --> 01:03:41,116
Do I look like
the settling-down kind?

1135
01:03:41,140 --> 01:03:42,737
Come on. Let me out.

1136
01:03:45,745 --> 01:03:48,290
I'll do the bending.
I'm dressed for it.

1137
01:03:48,314 --> 01:03:49,543
Thank you, Tommy.

1138
01:03:53,519 --> 01:03:55,750
Good night, buddy.
Good night, fella.

1139
01:05:26,679 --> 01:05:31,212
♪ Everywhere that beauty
goes you are ♪

1140
01:05:32,685 --> 01:05:38,400
♪ everywhere an orchid grows
you are ♪

1141
01:05:38,424 --> 01:05:41,270
♪ everything
that's young and gay ♪

1142
01:05:41,294 --> 01:05:44,673
♪ brighter than a holiday ♪

1143
01:05:44,697 --> 01:05:49,445
♪ everywhere
the angels play ♪

1144
01:05:49,469 --> 01:05:52,881
♪ you are ♪

1145
01:05:52,905 --> 01:05:56,919
♪ you're like Paris
in April and may ♪{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris}

1146
01:05:56,943 --> 01:06:01,457
♪ you're New York
on a silvery day ♪

1147
01:06:01,481 --> 01:06:05,694
♪ a Swiss alp
as the sun grows fainter ♪

1148
01:06:05,718 --> 01:06:09,865
♪ you're Loch Lomond
when Autumn is the painter ♪

1149
01:06:09,889 --> 01:06:13,902
♪ you're moonlight
on a night in Capri ♪

1150
01:06:13,926 --> 01:06:18,107
♪ and Cape Cod
looking out at the sea ♪

1151
01:06:18,131 --> 01:06:22,311
♪ you're all places
that leave me breathless ♪

1152
01:06:22,335 --> 01:06:23,912
♪ and no wonder ♪

1153
01:06:23,936 --> 01:06:27,902
♪ you're all the world
to me ♪

1154
01:09:38,130 --> 01:09:40,509
♪ Every night at 7:00 ♪

1155
01:09:42,001 --> 01:09:43,912
Come in!

1156
01:09:43,936 --> 01:09:46,181
Oh, morning. Oh, hello, Edgar.

1157
01:09:46,205 --> 01:09:49,318
Absolutely fantastic
notices, every one!

1158
01:09:49,342 --> 01:09:51,119
You can stay on for years.
Have you seen the dailies?

1159
01:09:51,143 --> 01:09:52,087
Rather!

1160
01:09:52,111 --> 01:09:53,689
Oh, we're so thrilled.

1161
01:09:53,713 --> 01:09:56,124
There's been an 8-week call
at the libraries.

1162
01:09:56,148 --> 01:09:57,192
There's been a what at the what?

1163
01:09:57,216 --> 01:09:58,694
The libraries, old boy.

1164
01:09:58,718 --> 01:10:00,529
That's what we call
our ticket brokers.

1165
01:10:00,553 --> 01:10:02,364
They want blocks of seats,
8 weeks in advance.

1166
01:10:02,388 --> 01:10:04,199
Why didn't you say so?

1167
01:10:04,223 --> 01:10:06,935
Oh, Tom, do you see the one
where they say we're brilliant?

1168
01:10:06,959 --> 01:10:08,136
Where?

1169
01:10:08,160 --> 01:10:09,538
Oh, Edgar, have you
called Irving yet?

1170
01:10:09,562 --> 01:10:10,724
I'm off to the office now.

1171
01:10:11,564 --> 01:10:13,709
I'll take it in my room.

1172
01:10:13,733 --> 01:10:15,711
Edgar, sit down a second, will ya?

1173
01:10:15,735 --> 01:10:18,146
I want you to do a favor for me.

1174
01:10:18,170 --> 01:10:19,314
Oh, at your command, old boy.

1175
01:10:19,338 --> 01:10:20,949
Look, when you call Irving,

1176
01:10:20,973 --> 01:10:23,118
ask him to find out
what happened to a boy

1177
01:10:23,142 --> 01:10:25,721
who used to work at Ogilvy's
department store in Chicago.

1178
01:10:25,745 --> 01:10:27,155
His name is, uh, Hal Rayton.

1179
01:10:27,179 --> 01:10:28,357
I've written it all down.

1180
01:10:28,381 --> 01:10:29,458
Oh, right you are.

1181
01:10:29,482 --> 01:10:31,193
Will you do that?

1182
01:10:31,217 --> 01:10:33,729
And, uh, don't say anything
to anybody about it, will ya?

1183
01:10:33,753 --> 01:10:35,364
It shall be graveyard, old boy,

1184
01:10:35,388 --> 01:10:36,565
graveyard.

1185
01:10:36,589 --> 01:10:37,791
Cheerio. All right. Thanks.

1186
01:10:39,191 --> 01:10:41,169
This is New York.
We're ready, London.

1187
01:10:41,193 --> 01:10:42,971
Mr. Irving Klinger
is on the line.

1188
01:10:42,995 --> 01:10:44,172
Hello, hello!

1189
01:10:44,196 --> 01:10:46,174
Irving there?
Edgar here.

1190
01:10:46,198 --> 01:10:47,542
Hey, Eddie, so how are things?

1191
01:10:47,566 --> 01:10:48,744
Buzzing right along.

1192
01:10:48,768 --> 01:10:50,379
A smash, huh?
So how were the notices?

1193
01:10:50,403 --> 01:10:52,180
Wizard, old boy, wizard!

1194
01:10:52,204 --> 01:10:53,382
That bad, huh?

1195
01:10:53,406 --> 01:10:54,583
No, no, no, no.

1196
01:10:54,607 --> 01:10:55,517
Wizard.

1197
01:10:55,541 --> 01:10:56,985
<i>Wiz-ard!</i>

1198
01:10:57,009 --> 01:10:58,186
Oh, wiz...

1199
01:10:58,210 --> 01:10:59,988
Yes.

1200
01:11:00,012 --> 01:11:01,990
Now, look,
Tom wants you to follow up on a chap

1201
01:11:02,014 --> 01:11:04,893
named Hal Rayton.
R-a-y-t-o-n.

1202
01:11:04,917 --> 01:11:07,396
Hal Rayton, huh?
What does he do, a single?

1203
01:11:07,420 --> 01:11:09,698
No, Mr. Rayton is not in
the theatrical profession.

1204
01:11:09,722 --> 01:11:11,199
He ain't in the theater,
so who needs him?

1205
01:11:11,223 --> 01:11:13,201
Oh, it's a personal matter of Tom's.

1206
01:11:13,225 --> 01:11:15,203
Now, when last heard from,
Mr. Rayton was employed

1207
01:11:15,227 --> 01:11:17,406
at Ogilvy's department
store in Chicago.

1208
01:11:17,430 --> 01:11:19,007
Ogilvy's. Dig ya.

1209
01:11:19,031 --> 01:11:21,209
I'll throw the hassle
to our Chicago branch,

1210
01:11:21,233 --> 01:11:23,612
have the whole mess in
your lap in a fast 2 days.

1211
01:11:23,636 --> 01:11:25,414
Oh, no, no. That
won't be necessary.

1212
01:11:25,438 --> 01:11:27,416
Just follow up on the Rayton
matter and let us know.

1213
01:11:27,440 --> 01:11:29,618
Natch. I just said...

1214
01:11:29,642 --> 01:11:31,620
pip, now.

1215
01:11:31,644 --> 01:11:33,622
Oh, uh, yes, um...

1216
01:11:33,646 --> 01:11:36,243
Uh, dig... You!

1217
01:11:39,652 --> 01:11:42,798
Oh! There's Jamie
for you.

1218
01:11:42,822 --> 01:11:45,801
Wouldn't you know he'd be
late, even to the palace!

1219
01:11:45,825 --> 01:11:48,236
Now, take it easy, mother.
Maybe the traffic was heavy.

1220
01:11:48,260 --> 01:11:50,238
Oh, it's not the traffic. It's Jamie.

1221
01:11:50,262 --> 01:11:52,607
Ever since I told him I don't
like to be kept waiting,

1222
01:11:52,631 --> 01:11:54,443
he's been keeping me waiting.

1223
01:11:54,467 --> 01:11:57,646
You know, mother,
there's an old Spanish proverb which goes,

1224
01:11:57,670 --> 01:11:59,548
"he who does not love the
faults of his loved one

1225
01:11:59,572 --> 01:12:01,249
does not love
at all."

1226
01:12:01,273 --> 01:12:02,818
Hmm. That may be well
and good for the Spanish,

1227
01:12:02,842 --> 01:12:04,052
but I'm English.

1228
01:12:04,076 --> 01:12:06,054
You know what I think? No, dearie.

1229
01:12:06,078 --> 01:12:08,056
I think he's all excited
about seeing you.

1230
01:12:08,080 --> 01:12:09,257
You think so?

1231
01:12:09,281 --> 01:12:10,459
How do I look?

1232
01:12:10,483 --> 01:12:12,728
As elegant as can be.

1233
01:12:12,752 --> 01:12:15,063
Tommy, tell him he's going too fast.

1234
01:12:15,087 --> 01:12:15,997
Driver, could you slow down a little?

1235
01:12:16,021 --> 01:12:17,466
Yes, sir.

1236
01:12:17,490 --> 01:12:18,834
What say we step off
and have a quick one?

1237
01:12:18,858 --> 01:12:20,469
To the health
of the royal couple, eh?

1238
01:12:20,493 --> 01:12:22,604
Sit back, Jamie. We're
not stopping anywhere.

1239
01:12:22,628 --> 01:12:24,272
Well, tell him to go a little slower.

1240
01:12:24,296 --> 01:12:25,741
He can't go any slower.
Don't be so nervous.

1241
01:12:25,765 --> 01:12:27,476
And when you see her,

1242
01:12:27,500 --> 01:12:28,844
be sure and act like the
gentleman you look like.

1243
01:12:28,868 --> 01:12:30,846
I will, but if she says
one word of criticism,

1244
01:12:30,870 --> 01:12:32,481
I'll hit her on the head
with this cap.

1245
01:12:32,505 --> 01:12:33,682
You'll do nothing of the kind.

1246
01:12:33,706 --> 01:12:35,027
Here we are.
Stop here, driver.

1247
01:12:40,312 --> 01:12:42,743
We'll walk the rest of the way.

1248
01:12:51,323 --> 01:12:54,302
Come on now. Don't lose
your nerve. Get in there.

1249
01:12:54,326 --> 01:12:55,522
Go on.

1250
01:12:58,731 --> 01:13:00,275
Hello, Jamie.

1251
01:13:00,299 --> 01:13:01,610
Good day, Sarah.

1252
01:13:01,634 --> 01:13:03,512
Jamie, your suit's handsome.

1253
01:13:03,536 --> 01:13:04,446
Handsome.

1254
01:13:04,470 --> 01:13:06,882
Thank you.

1255
01:13:06,906 --> 01:13:08,884
I'm, uh, I'm sorry I'm late.

1256
01:13:08,908 --> 01:13:10,485
The traffic was heavy today.

1257
01:13:10,509 --> 01:13:13,121
I told the cabby to go
as fast as he could.

1258
01:13:13,145 --> 01:13:14,723
Shall we go?

1259
01:13:15,748 --> 01:13:16,725
Allow me.

1260
01:13:16,749 --> 01:13:18,145
Jamie, don't.

1261
01:13:19,552 --> 01:13:21,329
I knew they'd hold.

1262
01:13:21,353 --> 01:13:23,550
There are some days when
you can do nothing wrong.

1263
01:13:25,357 --> 01:13:27,335
Do you think they'll stay together?

1264
01:13:27,359 --> 01:13:28,904
Sure.
I hope so.

1265
01:13:28,928 --> 01:13:30,739
For them as well as for me.

1266
01:13:30,763 --> 01:13:32,908
I could never have left mother alone.

1267
01:13:32,932 --> 01:13:34,876
Now you can get married
and forget about her.

1268
01:13:34,900 --> 01:13:36,563
Yes.

1269
01:13:38,370 --> 01:13:39,347
Yeah?

1270
01:13:39,371 --> 01:13:40,348
Good evening.

1271
01:13:40,372 --> 01:13:41,750
Edgar.

1272
01:13:41,774 --> 01:13:42,751
I have the information you requested

1273
01:13:42,775 --> 01:13:44,519
regarding
Mr. Rayton.

1274
01:13:44,543 --> 01:13:46,922
Ohh, what? Well,
it's nothing terribly exciting.

1275
01:13:46,946 --> 01:13:48,924
Mr. Rayton still
works at Ogilvy's.

1276
01:13:48,948 --> 01:13:50,926
He's still in the luggage department.

1277
01:13:50,950 --> 01:13:52,761
He used to live in Chicago proper,

1278
01:13:52,785 --> 01:13:54,729
but not long ago he and
his wife moved to Evanston.

1279
01:13:54,753 --> 01:13:56,398
His wife?!

1280
01:13:56,422 --> 01:13:58,099
Why, yes,
he was married several months ago.

1281
01:13:58,123 --> 01:14:00,101
My, my, my.

1282
01:14:00,125 --> 01:14:02,103
I say, who is this chap anyway?

1283
01:14:02,127 --> 01:14:04,105
Oh, uh, he was engaged
to a girl in the show.

1284
01:14:04,129 --> 01:14:06,107
I was just finding out
about him for her.

1285
01:14:06,131 --> 01:14:07,542
Oh, I say, that's frightful!

1286
01:14:07,566 --> 01:14:08,910
Yes, isn't it?

1287
01:14:08,934 --> 01:14:10,912
Well, what are you so pleased about?

1288
01:14:10,936 --> 01:14:12,347
I always smile when I'm heartbroken.

1289
01:14:12,371 --> 01:14:14,115
I better tell her.

1290
01:14:14,139 --> 01:14:15,784
Oh, not now.
Don't you think you should wait

1291
01:14:15,808 --> 01:14:16,885
till after the performance?

1292
01:14:16,909 --> 01:14:18,420
It would seem kinder.

1293
01:14:18,444 --> 01:14:20,922
Maybe you're right.
Edgar, you're a real pal.

1294
01:14:20,946 --> 01:14:22,390
I'd stick up for you anytime.

1295
01:14:22,414 --> 01:14:23,925
Well, thanks, old chap.

1296
01:14:23,949 --> 01:14:25,393
That's frightfully
decent of you to say.

1297
01:14:25,417 --> 01:14:27,395
Well, if you'll excuse me.

1298
01:14:27,419 --> 01:14:30,065
Oh! I shall be around to pick
you up early tomorrow morning.

1299
01:14:30,089 --> 01:14:31,132
What's tomorrow?

1300
01:14:31,156 --> 01:14:33,301
Why, the royal wedding.

1301
01:14:33,325 --> 01:14:34,870
And I shall take you someplace
where you'll see all the pageantry.

1302
01:14:34,894 --> 01:14:36,271
Great.

1303
01:14:36,295 --> 01:14:37,958
- Second act, Mr. Bowen.
- Right.

1304
01:14:57,850 --> 01:14:59,995
♪ I left my hat ♪

1305
01:15:00,019 --> 01:15:02,631
♪ in Haiti ♪

1306
01:15:02,655 --> 01:15:06,635
♪ in some forgotten flat
in Haiti ♪

1307
01:15:06,659 --> 01:15:09,004
♪ I couldn't tell you
how I got there ♪

1308
01:15:09,028 --> 01:15:11,940
♪ I only know it was so ♪

1309
01:15:11,964 --> 01:15:13,475
♪ hot there ♪

1310
01:15:13,499 --> 01:15:15,644
♪ she took my hat ♪

1311
01:15:15,668 --> 01:15:18,446
♪ politely ♪

1312
01:15:18,470 --> 01:15:22,350
♪ and wound her arms
around me tightly ♪

1313
01:15:22,374 --> 01:15:24,953
♪ but I remember
nothing clearly ♪

1314
01:15:24,977 --> 01:15:28,857
♪ except the flame
when she came near me ♪

1315
01:15:28,881 --> 01:15:31,660
♪ her eyes ♪

1316
01:15:31,684 --> 01:15:35,764
♪ had the fire
of surrender ♪

1317
01:15:35,788 --> 01:15:39,801
♪ and her touch,
it was tender ♪

1318
01:15:39,825 --> 01:15:42,671
♪ and I guess
in a moment as that ♪

1319
01:15:42,695 --> 01:15:44,472
♪ you forget
about your hat ♪

1320
01:15:44,496 --> 01:15:49,444
♪ so if you go to Haiti ♪

1321
01:15:49,468 --> 01:15:52,881
♪ there is a girl I know
in Haiti ♪

1322
01:15:52,905 --> 01:15:54,382
♪ if you can find her ♪

1323
01:15:54,406 --> 01:15:56,051
♪ you'll adore her ♪

1324
01:15:56,075 --> 01:15:58,486
♪ just look around
till you've found ♪

1325
01:15:58,510 --> 01:16:02,275
♪ someone who has
a blue-gray fedora ♪

1326
01:16:05,417 --> 01:16:07,295
♪ I think of
that gorgeous creature ♪

1327
01:16:07,319 --> 01:16:11,284
♪ when I'm all alone ♪

1328
01:16:13,325 --> 01:16:16,905
♪ whenever I do,
from down inside there comes ♪

1329
01:16:16,929 --> 01:16:20,026
♪ a groan ♪

1330
01:16:21,333 --> 01:16:23,912
♪ that son of a gun
in Haiti ♪

1331
01:16:23,936 --> 01:16:27,501
♪ has got
the prettiest hat I own ♪

1332
01:16:29,341 --> 01:16:31,086
♪ and when
it's bleak and chilly ♪

1333
01:16:31,110 --> 01:16:32,921
♪ and life is flat ♪

1334
01:16:32,945 --> 01:16:35,523
♪ I think of
that Haitian dilly ♪

1335
01:16:35,547 --> 01:16:38,345
♪ and think
I better go get my hat ♪

1336
01:17:22,161 --> 01:17:24,572
♪ I left my hat ♪

1337
01:17:24,596 --> 01:17:27,375
♪ in Haiti ♪

1338
01:17:27,399 --> 01:17:31,146
♪ in some forgotten flat
in Haiti ♪

1339
01:17:31,170 --> 01:17:33,782
♪ I couldn't tell you
how I got there ♪

1340
01:17:33,806 --> 01:17:36,584
♪ I only know it was so ♪

1341
01:17:36,608 --> 01:17:38,138
♪ hot there ♪

1342
01:17:46,418 --> 01:17:49,397
♪ But I remember
nothing clearly ♪

1343
01:17:49,421 --> 01:17:53,435
♪ except the flame
when she came near me ♪

1344
01:17:53,459 --> 01:17:56,404
♪ her eyes ♪

1345
01:17:56,428 --> 01:17:59,808
♪ had the fire
of surrender ♪

1346
01:17:59,832 --> 01:18:03,812
♪ and her touch,
it was tender ♪

1347
01:18:03,836 --> 01:18:07,015
♪ and I guess
in a moment as that ♪

1348
01:18:07,039 --> 01:18:08,616
♪ you forget
about your hat ♪

1349
01:18:08,640 --> 01:18:13,188
♪ so if you go to Haiti ♪

1350
01:18:13,212 --> 01:18:17,692
♪ there is a girl I know
in Haiti ♪

1351
01:18:17,716 --> 01:18:20,028
♪ if you can find her,
you'll adore her ♪

1352
01:18:20,052 --> 01:18:22,030
♪ just look around
till you've found ♪

1353
01:18:22,054 --> 01:18:25,819
♪ someone who has
a blue-gray fedora ♪

1354
01:21:00,312 --> 01:21:01,923
Ellen.

1355
01:21:01,947 --> 01:21:03,491
Ellen.

1356
01:21:03,515 --> 01:21:05,612
Got anybody to take you home?

1357
01:21:09,721 --> 01:21:11,084
Good.

1358
01:21:11,957 --> 01:21:12,967
Good night,
Mr. Bowen.

1359
01:21:12,991 --> 01:21:14,687
Good night.

1360
01:21:17,796 --> 01:21:19,841
Anne, I've something to tell you.

1361
01:21:19,865 --> 01:21:22,010
Yes.

1362
01:21:22,034 --> 01:21:24,646
I hope you won't think I've butted
in where I shouldn't have, but...

1363
01:21:24,670 --> 01:21:26,514
Jamie told me
your friend didn't call.

1364
01:21:27,739 --> 01:21:29,450
I didn't ask.
He told me.

1365
01:21:29,474 --> 01:21:31,986
I don't quite know why I did it,

1366
01:21:32,010 --> 01:21:34,555
but I decided to try and find
out what happened to him.

1367
01:21:34,579 --> 01:21:35,990
Did you?

1368
01:21:36,014 --> 01:21:37,191
Yes, I did.

1369
01:21:37,215 --> 01:21:38,726
Well?

1370
01:21:38,750 --> 01:21:40,561
Anne.

1371
01:21:40,585 --> 01:21:42,196
He's married.

1372
01:21:42,220 --> 01:21:43,798
Married?

1373
01:21:43,822 --> 01:21:45,385
I'm sorry.

1374
01:21:49,394 --> 01:21:50,972
How wonderful.

1375
01:21:50,996 --> 01:21:53,193
How simply wonderful.

1376
01:21:54,566 --> 01:21:56,744
Oh, Tom, thank you.

1377
01:21:56,768 --> 01:21:59,080
Oh, it was nothing.
Anne...

1378
01:21:59,104 --> 01:22:01,983
Here I've been worrying myself to
death over him on account of you,

1379
01:22:02,007 --> 01:22:04,385
and he's been married all the time.
Isn't it wonderful?

1380
01:22:04,409 --> 01:22:06,487
What do you mean, on account of me?

1381
01:22:06,511 --> 01:22:09,657
Well, on account of me being in love
with you and you being in love with me.

1382
01:22:09,681 --> 01:22:10,992
I'm in love with you?

1383
01:22:11,016 --> 01:22:12,545
Well, aren't you?

1384
01:22:15,187 --> 01:22:16,749
Yeah.

1385
01:22:19,191 --> 01:22:21,669
What a mess.

1386
01:22:21,693 --> 01:22:22,870
Why?

1387
01:22:22,894 --> 01:22:24,372
The next thing that happens,

1388
01:22:24,396 --> 01:22:25,573
we'll be thinking
about getting married.

1389
01:22:25,597 --> 01:22:27,008
I'm thinking about it right now.

1390
01:22:27,032 --> 01:22:28,376
There. You see?

1391
01:22:28,400 --> 01:22:30,378
Well, don't you want to marry me?

1392
01:22:30,402 --> 01:22:32,013
Yes, I do.

1393
01:22:32,037 --> 01:22:34,382
Anne, it isn't you.
It's marriage.

1394
01:22:34,406 --> 01:22:36,784
I'm afraid I couldn't be
married and make a go of it.

1395
01:22:36,808 --> 01:22:38,586
I've been living one way too long.

1396
01:22:38,610 --> 01:22:40,088
For me, it's always been work.

1397
01:22:40,112 --> 01:22:42,223
Everything else has
just been a side issue.

1398
01:22:42,247 --> 01:22:44,792
It isn't because I don't want to.
It's just that I...

1399
01:22:44,816 --> 01:22:46,627
I don't know if I could change.

1400
01:22:46,651 --> 01:22:48,363
Anne...

1401
01:22:48,387 --> 01:22:50,598
I know I'd be an awful
flop as a husband.

1402
01:22:50,622 --> 01:22:52,619
Do you understand?

1403
01:22:53,425 --> 01:22:56,037
I think I do, Tom.

1404
01:22:56,061 --> 01:22:57,405
What do we do?

1405
01:22:57,429 --> 01:22:59,626
Perhaps you better take me home.

1406
01:23:12,077 --> 01:23:14,255
Tom...

1407
01:23:14,279 --> 01:23:15,723
I only disagree with one thing.

1408
01:23:15,747 --> 01:23:17,225
What's that?

1409
01:23:17,249 --> 01:23:19,727
I think you'd make
a marvelous husband.

1410
01:23:19,751 --> 01:23:21,229
You do?

1411
01:23:21,253 --> 01:23:23,082
Yes, I do, Tom.

1412
01:23:49,481 --> 01:23:50,792
Hi.

1413
01:23:50,816 --> 01:23:52,260
Hi.

1414
01:23:52,284 --> 01:23:54,195
You're back early.

1415
01:23:54,219 --> 01:23:56,097
It isn't early.

1416
01:23:56,121 --> 01:23:57,865
I thought it was.

1417
01:23:57,889 --> 01:23:59,867
Well, it isn't.
It's late.

1418
01:23:59,891 --> 01:24:01,688
Is it?

1419
01:24:05,397 --> 01:24:07,694
I didn't say anything.

1420
01:24:10,502 --> 01:24:12,480
Tom, John wants to marry me.

1421
01:24:12,504 --> 01:24:14,115
He does?

1422
01:24:14,139 --> 01:24:15,950
That's funny.

1423
01:24:15,974 --> 01:24:18,486
Well, it might be to you,
but it's terribly serious to him.

1424
01:24:18,510 --> 01:24:20,688
He's very much in love with me,

1425
01:24:20,712 --> 01:24:22,490
and he said he's found
a new faith in everything

1426
01:24:22,514 --> 01:24:24,125
all on account of me.

1427
01:24:24,149 --> 01:24:25,493
How do you feel?

1428
01:24:25,517 --> 01:24:29,697
Well... very constructive.

1429
01:24:29,721 --> 01:24:31,699
I never thought
I'd live to see the day

1430
01:24:31,723 --> 01:24:33,701
you'd inspire anybody to do
anything but slug it out.

1431
01:24:33,725 --> 01:24:37,305
All right, go ahead, make fun of me.

1432
01:24:37,329 --> 01:24:38,906
I'm sorry, Ellie.

1433
01:24:38,930 --> 01:24:41,142
You really are involved
this time, aren't you?

1434
01:24:41,166 --> 01:24:42,343
Up to here.

1435
01:24:42,367 --> 01:24:44,145
What are you gonna do?

1436
01:24:44,169 --> 01:24:45,480
I don't know.

1437
01:24:45,504 --> 01:24:47,315
I was rather hoping

1438
01:24:47,339 --> 01:24:50,370
you'd make up my mind for
me like you usually do.

1439
01:24:51,543 --> 01:24:53,354
It sure is funny.

1440
01:24:53,378 --> 01:24:54,922
Why do you keep saying that

1441
01:24:54,946 --> 01:24:56,357
when it isn't funny at all?!

1442
01:24:56,381 --> 01:24:57,525
It's very important.

1443
01:24:57,549 --> 01:24:58,926
I know it is.

1444
01:24:58,950 --> 01:25:00,928
I only meant it's funny because...

1445
01:25:00,952 --> 01:25:03,731
Anne wants me to marry her.

1446
01:25:03,755 --> 01:25:04,984
She does?

1447
01:25:08,193 --> 01:25:09,937
My gosh.

1448
01:25:09,961 --> 01:25:11,439
It sure is funny, isn't it?

1449
01:25:11,463 --> 01:25:13,174
Why?

1450
01:25:13,198 --> 01:25:15,743
Well, I don't know.
It's just funny, that's all.

1451
01:25:15,767 --> 01:25:18,179
Now, what's so funny about
someone wanting to marry me?

1452
01:25:18,203 --> 01:25:20,181
I have a few good points, you know.

1453
01:25:20,205 --> 01:25:22,750
Tommy,
are you really in love with her?

1454
01:25:22,774 --> 01:25:23,751
Up to here.

1455
01:25:23,775 --> 01:25:26,053
Oh, my.

1456
01:25:26,077 --> 01:25:27,188
Well...

1457
01:25:27,212 --> 01:25:28,756
I wonder what would happen

1458
01:25:28,780 --> 01:25:30,758
if we just threw over
all our principles

1459
01:25:30,782 --> 01:25:32,193
and went ahead and
got married anyway.

1460
01:25:32,217 --> 01:25:33,394
You'd stay here
in England with John...

1461
01:25:33,418 --> 01:25:34,729
why would I stay here?

1462
01:25:34,753 --> 01:25:36,364
You can't go traipsing
all over the world

1463
01:25:36,388 --> 01:25:37,565
and leave your husband at home.

1464
01:25:37,589 --> 01:25:38,966
What kind of a marriage
would that be?

1465
01:25:38,990 --> 01:25:40,201
No, I...

1466
01:25:40,225 --> 01:25:41,969
I suppose not.

1467
01:25:41,993 --> 01:25:43,204
Well...

1468
01:25:43,228 --> 01:25:44,572
well, what would you do?

1469
01:25:44,596 --> 01:25:45,806
I'd marry Anne...

1470
01:25:45,830 --> 01:25:47,074
no, no, I mean who
would you dance with?

1471
01:25:47,098 --> 01:25:48,809
Well, I never thought of that.

1472
01:25:48,833 --> 01:25:50,578
Not having you,
I might try Anne if she wants to.

1473
01:25:50,602 --> 01:25:52,580
She loves to dance and
shows a lot of promise.

1474
01:25:52,604 --> 01:25:53,814
Do you really think so?

1475
01:25:53,838 --> 01:25:55,216
Yeah.

1476
01:25:55,240 --> 01:25:57,418
I thought she was a
little awkward myself.

1477
01:25:57,442 --> 01:25:58,586
So were you when you started.

1478
01:25:58,610 --> 01:25:59,987
Well, I know it,

1479
01:26:00,011 --> 01:26:01,789
but it takes years
to build a good team.

1480
01:26:01,813 --> 01:26:03,257
You've always said that.

1481
01:26:03,281 --> 01:26:04,792
And there's a lot
of excitement in doing it.

1482
01:26:04,816 --> 01:26:06,727
Well, it just isn't fair, Tom.
What isn't?

1483
01:26:06,751 --> 01:26:09,397
What? I'm getting the
short end of the stick.

1484
01:26:09,421 --> 01:26:11,399
You want me to settle back
and get out of the way

1485
01:26:11,423 --> 01:26:13,768
while you go off with someone
else and have all the fun.

1486
01:26:13,792 --> 01:26:15,770
I think it's rotten of
you, Tommy, simply rotten.

1487
01:26:15,794 --> 01:26:17,572
I didn't ask...
everything's been so perfect.

1488
01:26:17,596 --> 01:26:18,973
We've had a wonderful life together.

1489
01:26:18,997 --> 01:26:20,608
We've been very successful.

1490
01:26:20,632 --> 01:26:21,976
How can you even
consider breaking it up?

1491
01:26:22,000 --> 01:26:23,244
And breaking it up for what?

1492
01:26:23,268 --> 01:26:24,445
To get married.
Oh, no, Tom.

1493
01:26:24,469 --> 01:26:25,680
Marriage is a tough business.

1494
01:26:25,704 --> 01:26:27,582
You know, I've always thought marr...

1495
01:26:27,606 --> 01:26:29,250
responsibilities, obligations,
a home to take care of.

1496
01:26:29,274 --> 01:26:31,185
Maybe children to look after.

1497
01:26:31,209 --> 01:26:33,588
Imagine being married to
someone you're dancing with.

1498
01:26:33,612 --> 01:26:35,089
If either the marriage or
the dancing doesn't work out,

1499
01:26:35,113 --> 01:26:36,991
both of them go kaplooey!

1500
01:26:37,015 --> 01:26:39,260
Oh, no. No, Thomas.
It's a terrible chance to take.

1501
01:26:39,284 --> 01:26:41,429
It is, isn't it?

1502
01:26:41,453 --> 01:26:43,497
We mustn't do it.
We've slaved too hard to get where we are.

1503
01:26:43,521 --> 01:26:44,699
Why should we give it up?

1504
01:26:44,723 --> 01:26:46,133
It does seem foolish, doesn't it?

1505
01:26:46,157 --> 01:26:47,535
We're a team, and a darn good one.

1506
01:26:47,559 --> 01:26:49,270
We ought to stay that way.

1507
01:26:49,294 --> 01:26:51,706
Maybe you're right.
Well, of course I'm right.

1508
01:26:51,730 --> 01:26:53,341
Oh, you do see it, don't you, Tommy?

1509
01:26:53,365 --> 01:26:55,209
Sure I see it.

1510
01:26:55,233 --> 01:26:56,711
It's you and me,
just like it's always been.

1511
01:26:56,735 --> 01:26:58,713
I'm sure we're doing the right thing.

1512
01:26:58,737 --> 01:27:00,715
Thank you so much
for deciding for me.

1513
01:27:00,739 --> 01:27:03,169
Forget it.

1514
01:27:16,354 --> 01:27:18,499
♪ What a lovely day
for a wedding ♪

1515
01:27:18,523 --> 01:27:20,534
♪ happy are we to say ♪

1516
01:27:20,558 --> 01:27:22,637
♪ it's a lovely day
for a wedding ♪

1517
01:27:22,661 --> 01:27:24,538
♪ lovely in every way ♪

1518
01:27:24,562 --> 01:27:26,907
♪ there's families out,
without a doubt ♪

1519
01:27:26,931 --> 01:27:29,310
♪ to see what
all the joy's about ♪

1520
01:27:29,334 --> 01:27:32,913
♪ for everywhere the world
is merry and gay ♪

1521
